{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03419","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03419","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03419","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph","familyName":"Rawson","name":"Rawson, Joseph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Domniki","familyName":"Ladopoulou","name":"Ladopoulou, Domniki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Petros","familyName":"Dellaportas","name":"Dellaportas, Petros","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-task Neural Diffusion Processes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T18:26:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:05:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-16T12:40:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-19T01:34:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-15T12:14:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-16T01:10:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural diffusion processes provide a scalable, non-Gaussian approach to modelling distributions over functions, but existing formulations are limited to single-task inference and do not capture dependencies across related tasks. We propose multi-task neural diffusion processes (MT-NDPs), an extension that incorporates a task encoder to enable task-conditioned probabilistic regression and few-shot adaptation across related functions. The task encoder extracts a low-dimensional representation from context observations and conditions the diffusion model on this representation, allowing information sharing across tasks while accommodating variable-sized context sets and preserving permutation invariance with respect to the ordering of context observations. We evaluate MT-NDPs through synthetic experiments and two real-world applications involving wind turbine data and school examination outcomes. The results demonstrate improved predictive accuracy relative to single-task diffusion baselines when context observations are available, alongside competitive performance against neural-process and Gaussian-process alternatives. In the wind-energy application, MT-NDPs achieve the lowest average coverage error among the evaluated models at larger context sizes, demonstrating improved predictive uncertainty calibration. Overall, our findings highlight the potential of task-conditioned diffusion models for uncertainty-aware multi-task regression and few-shot adaptation to previously unseen tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"37 pages, 16 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03419","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:49:27Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:49:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-16T03:15:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04440","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04440","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04440","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farid","familyName":"Bozorgnia","name":"Bozorgnia, Farid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vyacheslav","familyName":"Kungurtsev","name":"Kungurtsev, Vyacheslav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shirali","familyName":"Kadyrov","name":"Kadyrov, Shirali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohsen","familyName":"Yousefnezhad","name":"Yousefnezhad, Mohsen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fractional Heat Kernel for Semi-Supervised Graph Learning with Small Training Sample Size"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T02:15:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:11:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-08T12:32:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-09T01:06:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop a source-driven fractional heat-kernel framework for semi-super\\-vised graph learning that combines nonlocal propagation with sustained label information. A fixed nonzero label source compatible with the Laplacian null space prevents asymptotic collapse into that space, providing a mechanism for mitigating oversmoothing at long diffusion times. The fractional order controls the relative modal attenuation and the spectral weighting of the sustained response, while the diffusion time sets the propagation horizon. We characterize conservation laws and equilibria on normalized and disconnected graphs, develop a null-space deflation, and analyze the approximation of the propagators. On Two-Moon, fractional orders improve source-free propagation, while compatible source-driven diffusion exceeds $96\\%$ mean accuracy with one training label per class at orders $0.8$ and $1$. On Cora and CiteSeer, the source-driven pipeline improves mean accuracy over GAT by $9.2$ and $8.0$ percentage points at one training label per class, with model selection on $500$ labeled validation nodes and closely comparable classical and fractional pipeline configurations; it matches GAT on PubMed and trails it at ten and twenty labels per class on Cora. Within GraphHeat, validation-based exponent selection at a common diffusion time yields a paired gain of $1.45$ percentage points at one label per class."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04440","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:14:34Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:14:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-09T03:15:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03997","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03997","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03997","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Junjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arshana","familyName":"Welivita","name":"Welivita, Arshana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeleikun","familyName":"Di","name":"Di, Zeleikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingfu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jingfu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuzhe","familyName":"Zhi","name":"Zhi, Xuzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ritu","familyName":"Agarwal","name":"Agarwal, Ritu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gordon","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Gordon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mapping Patient-Perceived Physician Traits from Nationwide Online Reviews with LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T02:16:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T15:01:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:53:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding how patients perceive their physicians is essential to improving trust, communication, and satisfaction. Patients increasingly consult large language models (LLMs) to summarize physician reviews and shape provider choices, yet the national landscape of patient-perceived physician traits remains poorly characterized. We present an LLM-based pipeline that extracts ten patient-perceived physician trait scores from review text: five Big-Five-style and five patient-oriented dimensions. From one million U.S. physicians, we analyze 4.1 million reviews of 226,999 physicians. We validate the pipeline through multi-model comparison and human expert benchmarking. LLM and human-rater trait scores from reviews are consistent. Trait scores correlate strongly with review rating scores yet retain substantial independent variance. Two national-scale patterns emerge: male physicians receive higher trait scores across all traits, with the largest gap in clinical competence; specialty differences are driven by encounter context, with surgical specialties leading interpersonal qualities and psychiatry lowest. Cluster analysis identifies four physician archetypes, from \"Uniform High\" (33.8%, high across traits) to \"Uniform Low\" (22.6%, low across traits). This map of LLM-derived physician traits exposes how LLMs read the U.S. clinical workforce. Pending clinical validation, it opens future research on fairness, bias, and how LLM-mediated provider search shapes patient choice."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in npj Digital Medicine"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03997","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:40Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-07T02:15:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03434","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03434","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03434","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiying","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Zhiying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raihan","familyName":"Seraj","name":"Seraj, Raihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcos","familyName":"Villagra","name":"Villagra, Marcos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bidhan","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Bidhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Paris: A Decentralized Trained Open-Weight Diffusion Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T18:53:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:06:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-12T03:17:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T02:02:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-31T16:40:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-03T00:57:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Paris, the first publicly released diffusion model pre-trained entirely through decentralized computation. Paris demonstrates that high-quality text-to-image generation can be achieved without centrally coordinated infrastructure. Paris is open for research and commercial use. Paris required implementing our Distributed Diffusion Training framework from scratch. The model consists of 8 expert diffusion models (129M-605M parameters each) trained in complete isolation with no gradient, parameter, or intermediate activation synchronization. Rather than requiring synchronized gradient updates across thousands of GPUs, we partition data into semantically coherent clusters where each expert independently optimizes its subset while collectively approximating the full distribution. A lightweight transformer router dynamically selects appropriate experts at inference, achieving generation quality comparable to centrally coordinated baselines. Eliminating synchronization enables training on heterogeneous hardware without specialized interconnects. Empirical validation confirms that Paris's decentralized training maintains generation quality while removing the dedicated GPU cluster requirement for large-scale diffusion models. Paris achieves this using 14$\\times$ less training data and 16$\\times$ less compute than the prior decentralized baseline."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03434","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:49:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:49:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-03T02:08:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04019","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04019","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04019","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anthony","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Anthony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Simple Policy Gradients for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T03:53:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:47:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-01T03:54:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T01:59:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-23T05:31:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-24T00:25:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion large language models (dLLMs) represent a promising alternative to autoregressive LLMs; however, the lack of effective post-training techniques, including reinforcement learning (RL), remains a key challenge for dLLMs, especially for downstream applications. Existing approaches often rely on a sequence-level view that requires biased likelihood approximations. In this work, we propose Amortized Group Relative Policy Optimization (AGRPO), a policy gradient algorithm that leverages the Markovian nature of dLLMs, optimizing individual denoising steps rather than full sequences. Our approach improves alignment between the trained policy and the inference process and also admits efficient, unbiased gradient updates via a novel timestep estimation scheme. We demonstrate AGRPO's effectiveness on different math and reasoning tasks, achieving absolute accuracy gains of +59.4\\% and +69.7\\% on Countdown and Sudoku over the base LLaDA model, exceeding comparable methods such as diffu-GRPO. Code is available at https://github.com/probablyabot/agrpo."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages. ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04019","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:13Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-24T02:11:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04487","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04487","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04487","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Willa","familyName":"Potosnak","name":"Potosnak, Willa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Malcolm","familyName":"Wolff","name":"Wolff, Malcolm","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengfei","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Mengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruijun","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Ruijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatiana","familyName":"Konstantinova","name":"Konstantinova, Tatiana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dmitry","familyName":"Efimov","name":"Efimov, Dmitry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael W.","familyName":"Mahoney","name":"Mahoney, Michael W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boris","familyName":"Oreshkin","name":"Oreshkin, Boris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kin G.","familyName":"Olivares","name":"Olivares, Kin G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Forking-Sequences: Statistically and Computationally Efficient Multi-Horizon Forecasting with Reduced Volatility"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T04:51:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:14:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-16T00:31:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-17T01:12:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-17T20:50:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-19T01:09:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-08T17:43:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-09T01:56:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-10T03:07:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-13T00:14:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While accuracy is a critical requirement for time series forecasting, an equally important desideratum is reasonable forecast volatility across forecast creation dates (FCDs). Even highly accurate models can produce erratic revisions between FCDs, undermining trust and disrupting downstream decision-making. To improve the volatility of forecast revisions, state-of-the-art models like MQCNN, MQT, and SPADE employ a powerful yet underexplored neural network architectural design: forking-sequences. This architectural design jointly encodes and decodes the entire time series across all FCDs, producing an entire multi-horizon forecast grid in a single forward pass. This approach contrasts with conventional neural forecasting methods that process FCDs independently, generating only a single multi-horizon forecast per forward pass. In this work, we formalize the forking-sequences design and motivate its broader adoption by introducing a metric for quantifying excess volatility in forecast revisions and by providing theoretical and empirical analysis. We theoretically motivate three key benefits of forking-sequences: (i) reduced forecast volatility through ensembling; (ii) gradient variance reduction, improving the statistical efficiency of the training procedure; and (iii) improved inference computational efficiency. We validate the benefits of forking-sequences compared to baseline window-sampling on the M-series benchmark, using 16 datasets from the M1, M3, M4, and Tourism competitions. We observe median sCRPS improvements across datasets of 46.2%, 49.3%, 28.6%, 24.7%, and 6.4% for RNN, LSTM, CNN, Transformer, and State Space-based architectures, respectively. We then show that forecast ensembling during inference can reduce median forecast volatility by 13.2%, 13.0%, 10.9%, 10.2%, and 11.2% for these respective models trained with forking-sequences, while maintaining accuracy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in Transactions on Machine Learning Research"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04487","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:43Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-13T01:48:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04547","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04547","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04547","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghyeon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Seunghyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taesun","familyName":"Yeom","name":"Yeom, Taesun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinho","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jinho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wonpyo","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Wonpyo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyuyeun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Kyuyeun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaeho","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jaeho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Activation Quantization of Vision Encoders Needs Prefixing Registers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T07:27:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:18:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T06:33:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:27:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-28T12:54:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-01T02:26:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-19T12:18:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-20T00:55:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-08T06:28:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-09T00:24:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large pretrained vision encoders are central to multimodal intelligence, powering applications from on-device vision processing to vision-language models. Since these applications often demand real-time processing of massive visual data, reducing the inference cost of vision encoders is critical. Quantization offers a practical path, but it remains challenging even at 8-bit precision due to so-called outliers. In this work, we propose $\\textit{RegCache}$, a training-free algorithm that mitigates outliers in large-scale pretrained vision encoders and serves as a plug-in module that can be applied on top of other quantization methods. RegCache introduces outlier-prone yet semantically meaningless prefix tokens to the vision encoder, which prevent other tokens from having outliers. Notably, we observe that outliers in vision encoders behave differently from those in language models, motivating two technical innovations: middle-layer prefixing and token deletion. Experimental results show that our method consistently improves quantized model performance across various vision encoders, particularly in extremely low-bit regimes (e.g., 4-bit)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ECCV 2026. Code: https://github.com/spbob0418/RegCache"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04547","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-09T02:02:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04391","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04391","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04391","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saurabh","familyName":"Ranjan","name":"Ranjan, Saurabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Odegaard","name":"Odegaard, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Psychological Imagination Networks Show Cross-Population Centrality and Clustering Alignment in Humans That Large Language Models Fail to Replicate"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T23:01:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:08:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-06T19:24:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-10T01:02:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-07T07:07:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:45:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-10T02:12:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-11T01:18:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-01T18:39:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-03T00:05:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mental imagery vividness is a stable individual trait, yet whether imagined scenarios share relational structure across human and synthetic large language model (LLM) populations remains unknown. We applied psychological network analysis to vividness ratings from two validated questionnaires: the Vividness of Visual Imagery Questionnaire (VVIQ-2) and the Plymouth Sensory Imagery Questionnaire (PSIQ), across geographically and linguistically distinct human samples (Florida, Poland, and London; total N = 2,743) and six large language models (LLMs; Gemma3-12B/27B, their quantization-aware counterparts, Llama3.3-70B, and Llama4-16x17B). Imagination networks were constructed as regularized partial correlation graphs, with node centrality and community structure compared across populations using Pearson correlations and the Adjusted Rand Index (ARI). Human networks showed robust cross-population centrality correlations for expected influence, strength, and closeness (r = 0.31-0.93), and community detection recovered clusters aligned with VVIQ-2 scene contexts (ARI = 0.27-0.40) and PSIQ sensory modalities (ARI = 0.87-1.0). Betweenness centrality was unstable across all populations, consistent with its sensitivity to individual experiential history. LLMs failed to replicate human network structure: LLM-human centrality correlations were weak and largely non-significant after correction, and most LLM configurations produced degenerate single-cluster topologies (median ARI = 0). This failure was consistent across model architectures, parameter scales (12B-272B), and conversational conditions. We posit that these findings may be driven by human imagination networks reflecting memory organization accumulated through embodied experience, a representational structure that linguistic training alone does not reproduce regardless of model scale and conversational memory."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04391","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:13:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:13:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-03T02:13:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03243","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03243","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03243","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiheng","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Yiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yihe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Dearing","name":"Dearing, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuping","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Yuping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael E.","familyName":"Papka","name":"Papka, Michael E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiling","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Zhiling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ranking Before Serving: Low-Latency LLM Serving via Pairwise Learning-to-Rank"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-25T07:26:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:00:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T04:42:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:23:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-26T09:26:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-29T00:35:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.23919/isc.2026.11520485","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Efficient scheduling of large language model (LLM) inference tasks is critical for achieving low latency and high throughput, a challenge that is becoming increasingly acute with the rise of reasoning-capable LLMs whose generation lengths are highly variable. Traditional strategies like First Come, First-Serve (FCFS) often suffer from Head-of-Line (HOL) blocking, where long-running tasks delay shorter ones queued behind them. In this paper, we introduce PARS, a prompt-aware LLM task scheduler that mitigates HOL blocking by approximating shortest-job-first (SJF) scheduling through pairwise ranking with a margin ranking loss. PARS effectively predicts response-length-based task ordering directly from prompts, thereby optimizing scheduling decisions with minimal overhead. In addition, it integrates seamlessly with vLLM, a state-of-the-art LLM serving system, for the research community. Extensive experiments across multiple LLM models and real-world inference use cases, including chat, math, and code generation, demonstrate that PARS significantly reduces latency by up to 15.7x compared to the vLLM default scheduler. Cross-model evaluations demonstrate that our design generalizes effectively, allowing effective scheduling across diverse LLMs without requiring model-specific retraining."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 4 figures. Published in ISC High Performance 2026 Research Paper Proceedings (41st International Conference)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03243","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:09Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-29T01:59:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04767","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04767","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04767","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wonjun","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Wonjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Galim","name":"Galim, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghyuk","familyName":"Oh","name":"Oh, Seunghyuk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minjae","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Minjae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuibai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuibai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Coleman","familyName":"Hooper","name":"Hooper, Coleman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuezhou","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yuezhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyung Il","familyName":"Koo","name":"Koo, Hyung Il","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nam Ik","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Nam Ik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangwook","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Kangwook","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ParallelBench: Understanding the Trade-offs of Parallel Decoding in Diffusion LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T12:41:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:31:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-23T05:37:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-24T00:28:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While most autoregressive LLMs are constrained to one-by-one decoding, diffusion LLMs (dLLMs) have attracted growing interest for their potential to dramatically accelerate inference through parallel decoding. Despite this promise, the conditional independence assumption in dLLMs causes parallel decoding to ignore token dependencies, inevitably degrading generation quality when these dependencies are strong. However, existing works largely overlook these inherent challenges, and evaluations on standard benchmarks (e.g., math and coding) are not sufficient to capture the quality degradation caused by parallel decoding. To address this gap, we first provide an information-theoretic analysis of parallel decoding. We then conduct case studies on analytically tractable synthetic list operations from both data distribution and decoding strategy perspectives, offering quantitative insights that highlight the fundamental limitations of parallel decoding. Building on these insights, we propose ParallelBench, the first benchmark specifically designed for dLLMs, featuring realistic tasks that are trivial for humans and autoregressive LLMs yet exceptionally challenging for dLLMs under parallel decoding. Using ParallelBench, we systematically analyze both dLLMs and autoregressive LLMs, revealing that: (i) dLLMs under parallel decoding can suffer dramatic quality degradation in real-world scenarios, and (ii) current parallel decoding strategies struggle to adapt their degree of parallelism based on task difficulty, thus failing to achieve meaningful speedup without compromising quality. Our findings underscore the pressing need for innovative decoding methods that can overcome the current speed-quality trade-off. We release our benchmark to help accelerate the development of truly efficient dLLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026. Project Page: https://parallelbench.github.io"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04767","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:22:33Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:22:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-24T02:27:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04533","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04533","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04533","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunmin","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Hyunmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donghoon","familyName":"Ahn","name":"Ahn, Donghoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Susung","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Susung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jee Eun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jee Eun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seungryong","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Seungryong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyong Hwan","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Kyong Hwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TAG: Tangential Amplifying Guidance for Hallucination-Resistant Sampling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T06:53:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:17:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-26T13:18:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-27T00:59:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models achieve state-of-the-art image generation but often produce semantic inconsistencies, or hallucinations. Existing inference-time guidance methods rely on external signals or architectural modifications, adding computational overhead. We propose $\\mathbf{T}$angential $\\mathbf{A}$mplifying $\\mathbf{G}$uidance $\\mathbf{(TAG)}$, a training-free, architecture-agnostic, plug-and-play guidance method that operates purely on trajectory signals. TAG uses an intermediate sample as a projection basis and amplifies the tangential components of the estimated score to correct the sampling trajectory. A first-order Taylor analysis shows that this steers the state toward higher-probability regions of the data manifold, reducing inconsistencies and improving fidelity while adding negligible overhead to existing samplers. Code is available at our Project Page (https://hyeon-cho.github.io/TAG/)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICML 2026 (Regular)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04533","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:50Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-27T02:36:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03352","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03352","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03352","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahdi","familyName":"Farahbakhsh","name":"Farahbakhsh, Mahdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vishnu Teja","familyName":"Kunde","name":"Kunde, Vishnu Teja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dileep","familyName":"Kalathil","name":"Kalathil, Dileep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krishna","familyName":"Narayanan","name":"Narayanan, Krishna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jean-Francois","familyName":"Chamberland","name":"Chamberland, Jean-Francois","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Inference-Time Search Using Side Information for Diffusion-Based Image Reconstruction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T20:16:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:03:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-17T17:13:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-20T01:06:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T02:33:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-27T00:23:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have been used as priors for solving inverse problems. However, existing approaches typically overlook side information that could significantly improve reconstruction quality, especially in severely ill-posed settings. In this work, we propose a novel framework that incorporates side information into existing diffusion-based inverse problem solvers via inference-time search, in a plug-and-play, training-free manner. Through extensive experiments across a range of inverse problems, including inpainting, super-resolution, and several deblurring tasks, and across multiple diffusion-based inverse problem solvers (DPS, DAPS, and MPGD), we show that augmenting each solver with our framework consistently improves the quality of the reconstructions over the corresponding original method. To demonstrate the generality of our approach, we consider diverse forms of side information, including reference images, textual descriptions, and anatomical MRI scans. The code is available at this \\href{https://github.com/mahdi-farahbakhsh/DISS}{repository}\\footnote{https://github.com/mahdi-farahbakhsh/DISS}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03352","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:47:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:47:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-27T02:36:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03589","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03589","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03589","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ankit","familyName":"Bhardwaj","name":"Bhardwaj, Ankit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ananth","familyName":"Balashankar","name":"Balashankar, Ananth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lakshminarayanan","familyName":"Subramanian","name":"Subramanian, Lakshminarayanan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FieldFormer: Locality-Aware Transformers for Spatio-Temporal Modeling on Sparse Sensor Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T00:37:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:16:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-18T18:07:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-20T00:05:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spatio-temporal sensor data in real-world systems is often sparse, noisy, and irregular, making latent field reconstruction fundamentally underconstrained. Under extreme sparsity, multiple physically plausible fields may remain consistent with the same observations, requiring models to rely on inductive biases about locality, transport, and spatial regularity. In such regimes, reliable reconstruction is concentrated around the observational support induced by the sensor network, making sensor-space modeling a more identifiable objective than unconstrained global field recovery.\n We introduce FieldFormer, a mesh-free transformer architecture for locality-aware sensor-space modeling in persistent sensor networks. For each query, FieldFormer aggregates local evidence using learnable velocity-scaled offsets that adapt neighborhood geometry to spatio-temporal dependencies. Neighborhoods are constructed as fixed maximal sparse contexts over nearby sensors and bounded temporal windows, enabling stable and scalable inference under extreme sparsity. A local transformer encoder integrates neighborhood information, while a coordinate-based neural field formulation supports mesh-free prediction.\n We evaluate FieldFormer on five synthetic and real-world benchmarks, including anisotropic heat diffusion, shallow-water dynamics, atmospheric transport, and pollution monitoring datasets. Results show that locality-aware reconstruction provides strong advantages when local domains of dependence remain observed, enabling FieldFormer to consistently outperform state-of-the-art baselines on sparse sensor-space prediction tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03589","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:53:35Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:53:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-20T02:45:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04309","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04309","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04309","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dung V.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Dung V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hieu M.","familyName":"Vu","name":"Vu, Hieu M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nhi Y.","familyName":"Pham","name":"Pham, Nhi Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tan M.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Tan M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Activation Steering with a Feedback Controller"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T18:05:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:03:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-27T06:33:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-30T00:29:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-16T04:44:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-19T00:29:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Controlling the behaviors of large language models (LLM) is fundamental to their safety alignment and reliable deployment. However, existing steering methods are primarily driven by empirical insights and lack theoretical performance guarantees. In this work, we develop a control-theoretic foundation for activation steering by showing that popular steering methods correspond to the proportional (P) controllers, with the steering vector serving as the feedback signal. Building on this finding, we propose Proportional-Integral-Derivative (PID) Steering, a principled framework that leverages the full PID controller for activation steering in LLMs. The proportional (P) term aligns activations with target semantic directions, the integral (I) term accumulates errors to enforce persistent corrections across layers, and the derivative (D) term mitigates overshoot by counteracting rapid activation changes. This closed-loop design yields interpretable error dynamics and connects activation steering to classical stability guarantees in control theory. Moreover, PID Steering is lightweight, modular, and readily integrates with state-of-the-art steering methods. Extensive experiments across multiple LLM families and benchmarks demonstrate that PID Steering consistently outperforms existing approaches, achieving more robust and reliable behavioral control. The code is publicly available at: https://github.com/dungnvnus/pid-steering"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages in the main text. ICLR2026 Poster"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04309","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:11:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:11:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-19T04:48:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04233","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04233","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04233","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junheng","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Junheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qitian","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Qitian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PAINET: A Principled Efficient Transformer for 3D Dynamics Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T14:48:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:58:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-09T23:49:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T01:00:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modeling 3D dynamics is a fundamental problem in multi-body systems across scientific and engineering domains and has important practical implications in object trajectory prediction and simulation. While recent GNN-based approaches have achieved strong performance by enforcing geometric symmetries, encoding high-order features or incorporating neural-ODE mechanics, they typically depend on explicitly observed structures and inherently fail to capture the unobserved interactions that are crucial to complex physical behaviors and dynamics mechanism. In this paper, we propose PAINET, a principled SE(3)-equivariant transformer for learning all-pair interactions in multi-body systems. The model comprises: (1) a novel physics-inspired attention network derived from the minimization trajectory of an energy function, and (2) a parallel decoder that preserves equivariance while enabling efficient inference. Empirical results on diverse real-world benchmarks, including human motion capture, molecular dynamics, and large-scale protein simulations, show that PAINET consistently outperforms recently proposed models, yielding 4.7% to 41.5% error reductions in 3D dynamics prediction with comparable computation costs in terms of time and memory. Our codes, baseline models and datasets are available at https://github.com/Icarus1411/PAINET."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04233","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:09:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:09:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-12T05:03:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03761","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03761","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03761","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard A.","familyName":"Dubniczky","name":"Dubniczky, Richard A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bertalan","familyName":"Borsos","name":"Borsos, Bertalan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tamas","familyName":"Bisztray","name":"Bisztray, Tamas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Norbert","familyName":"Tihanyi","name":"Tihanyi, Norbert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"You Have Been LaTeXpOsEd: A Systematic Analysis of Information Leakage in Preprint Archives Using Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T10:03:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:30:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T10:09:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T01:59:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The widespread use of preprint repositories such as arXiv has accelerated the communication of scientific results but also introduced overlooked security risks. Beyond PDFs, these platforms provide unrestricted access to original source materials, including LaTeX sources, auxiliary code, figures, and embedded comments. In the absence of sanitization, submissions may disclose sensitive information that adversaries can harvest using open-source intelligence. In this work, we present the first large-scale security audit of preprint archives, analyzing more than 1.2 TB of source data from 100,000 arXiv submissions. We introduce LaTeXpOsEd, a four-stage framework that integrates pattern matching, logical filtering, traditional harvesting techniques, and large language models (LLMs) to uncover hidden disclosures within non-referenced files and LaTeX comments. To evaluate LLMs' secret-detection capabilities, we introduce LLMSec-DB, a benchmark on which we tested 25 state-of-the-art models. Our analysis uncovered thousands of PII leaks, GPS-tagged EXIF files, publicly available Google Drive and Dropbox folders, editable private SharePoint links, exposed GitHub and Google credentials, and cloud API keys. We also uncovered confidential author communications, internal disagreements, and conference submission credentials, exposing information that poses serious reputational risks to both researchers and institutions. We urge the research community and repository operators to take immediate action to close these hidden security gaps. To support open science, we release all scripts and methods from this study but withhold sensitive findings that could be misused, in line with ethical principles. The source code and related material are available at the project website https://github.com/LaTeXpOsEd"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03761","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:57:48Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:57:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-12T05:03:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04166","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04166","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04166","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco Edoardo","familyName":"Palma","name":"Palma, Marco Edoardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pooja","familyName":"Rani","name":"Rani, Pooja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harald C.","familyName":"Gall","name":"Gall, Harald C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi Language Models for On-the-Fly Syntax Highlighting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T11:48:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:55:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-04T18:14:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-06T00:03:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Syntax highlighting is a critical feature in modern software development environments, enhancing code readability and developer productivity. However, delivering accurate highlighting in real time remains challenging for online and web-based development tools due to strict time and memory constraints on backend services. These systems must serve highlights rapidly and frequently, even when code is partially valid or invalid. This has led to on-the-fly syntax highlighting, where visual annotations are generated just before content is served, often at high request rates and under incomplete input conditions. To meet these demands efficiently, state-of-the-art models use deep learning to learn the behavior of brute-force syntax highlighting resolvers, tools that are easy to implement but too slow for production. Through the Deep Abstraction process, brute-force strategies are encoded into fast statistical models that achieve both high accuracy and low-latency inference. Despite their success, such models face key challenges: they support only one programming language per model, require large datasets from slow brute-force generators, and involve resource-intensive training. In multi-language environments, this means maintaining multiple independent models, increasing system complexity and operational cost. This work addresses these issues by introducing a unified model capable of highlighting up to six mainstream programming languages, reducing deployment complexity by a factor of six and improving performance on unseen languages. A novel normalization technique significantly enhances model generalization, while few-shot learning experiments show that a small number of oracle samples can replace large datasets, minimizing dependence on brute-force generators. Combined, these innovations enable efficient, scalable, and cost-effective syntax highlighting across diverse programming languages."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04166","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:50Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-06T02:14:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04573","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04573","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04573","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoqiang","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Haoqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikki Lijing","familyName":"Kuang","name":"Kuang, Nikki Lijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicklas","familyName":"Majamaki","name":"Majamaki, Nicklas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Navdeep","familyName":"Jaitly","name":"Jaitly, Navdeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-An","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yi-An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lianhui","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Lianhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LaDiR: Latent Diffusion Enhances LLMs for Text Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:15:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T17:58:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:03:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T07:01:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:35:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-11T22:01:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-15T01:09:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-03T09:27:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:38:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-23T01:00:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-04-24T00:15:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate their reasoning ability through chain-of-thought (CoT) generation. However, LLM's autoregressive decoding may limit the ability to revisit and refine earlier tokens in a holistic manner, which can also lead to inefficient exploration for diverse solutions. In this paper, we propose LaDiR} (Latent Diffusion Reasoner), a novel reasoning framework that unifies the expressiveness of continuous latent representation with the iterative refinement capabilities of latent diffusion models for an existing LLM. We first construct a structured latent reasoning space using a Variational Autoencoder (VAE) that encodes text reasoning steps into blocks of thought tokens, preserving semantic information and interpretability while offering compact but expressive representations. Subsequently, we utilize a latent diffusion model that learns to denoise a block of latent thought tokens with a blockwise bidirectional attention mask, enabling longer horizon and iterative refinement with adaptive test-time compute. This design, combined with explicit diversity guidance during diffusion inference, enables the generation of multiple diverse reasoning trajectories that explore distinct regions of the latent space, rather than producing repetitive solutions as often occurs in standard autoregressive sampling. We conduct evaluations on a suite of mathematical reasoning, code generation and puzzle planning benchmarks. Empirical results show that LaDiR consistently improves accuracy, diversity, and interpretability over existing autoregressive, diffusion-based, and latent reasoning methods, revealing a new paradigm for text reasoning with latent diffusion."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04573","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-24T02:13:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04595","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04595","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04595","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yulong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianxiong","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jianxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianguo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhichao","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Zhichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bojun","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Bojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luziwei","familyName":"Leng","name":"Leng, Luziwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SpikingMamba: Towards Energy-Efficient Large Language Models via Knowledge Distillation from Mamba"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:49:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:20:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-12T08:00:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T00:44:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable performance across tasks but remain energy-intensive due to dense matrix operations. Spiking neural networks (SNNs) improve energy efficiency by replacing dense matrix multiplications with sparse accumulations. Their sparse spike activity enables efficient LLMs deployment on edge devices. However, prior SNN-based LLMs often sacrifice performance for efficiency, and recovering accuracy typically requires full pretraining, which is costly and impractical. To address this, we propose SpikingMamba, an energy-efficient SNN-based LLMs distilled from Mamba that improves energy efficiency with minimal accuracy sacrifice. SpikingMamba integrates two key components: (a) SI-LIF, a signed-integer spiking neuron that preserves semantic polarity through signed multi-level spike representations. (b) A training-exclusive Smoothed Gradient Compensation (SGC) path mitigating quantization loss while preserving spike-driven efficiency. We employ a single-stage distillation strategy to transfer the zero-shot ability of pretrained Mamba and further enhance it via reinforcement learning (RL). Experiments show that SpikingMamba-1.3B achieves a 4.76$\\times$ energy benefit, with only a 4.78\\% zero-shot accuracy gap compared to the original Mamba. The model achieves a further 2.55\\% accuracy improvement after RL, narrowing the performance gap from 4.78\\% to 2.23\\%. Code is available at: https://github.com/HuuYuLong/SpikingMamba ."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04595","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:20Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:50:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04116","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04116","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04116","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziying","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ziying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaqing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yaqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quanming","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Quanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Searching Meta Reasoning Skeleton to Guide LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T09:36:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:53:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-27T02:52:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-01T01:17:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-28T09:12:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-31T00:29:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-16T14:08:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-17T00:52:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Meta reasoning behaviors work as a skeleton to guide large language model (LLM) reasoning, thus help to improve reasoning performance. However, prior researches implement meta reasoning skeleton with manually designed structure, limiting ability to adapt to query-specific requirement and capture intricate logical dependency among reasoning steps. To deal with the challenges, we represent meta reasoning skeleton with directed acyclic graph (DAG) to unify skeletons proposed in prior works and model intricate logical dependency. Then we propose AutoMR, a framework that searches for query-aware meta reasoning skeleton automatically inspired by automated machine learning (AutoML). Specifically, we construct search space based on DAG representation of skeleton and then formulate the search problem. We design a dynamic skeleton sampling algorithm by expanding meta reasoning skeleton along with reasoning context at inference time. This algorithm can derive any meta reasoning skeleton in search space efficiently and adapt skeleton to evolving base reasoning context, thus enable efficient query-aware skeleton search. We conduct experiments on extensive benchmark datasets. Experimental results show that AutoMR achieves better reasoning performance than previous works broadly."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04116","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:06:37Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:06:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-17T02:18:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04601","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04601","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04601","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guochen","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Guochen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luyuan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Luyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingni","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Qingni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuejian","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Yuejian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhonghai","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhonghai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:06:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:21:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T12:26:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:44:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-25T07:11:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-26T00:33:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The current paradigm of training large language models (LLMs) on public available Web data is becoming unsustainable as high-quality data sources in specialized domains near exhaustion. Federated Learning (FL) emerges as a practical solution for the next generation of AI on a decentralized Web, enabling privacy-preserving collaborative fine-tuning on decentralized private data. While Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) is standard for efficient fine-tuning, its federated application faces a critical bottleneck: communication overhead under heterogeneous network conditions. Structural redundancy in LoRA parameters increases communication costs and causes aggregation conflicts. To address this, we propose FedSRD, a Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose framework for communication-efficient federated LLM fine-tuning. We introduce importance-aware sparsification to reduce the upload parameter count while preserving the structural integrity of LoRA updates. The server aggregates updates in full-rank space to mitigate conflicts, then decomposes the global update into a sparse low-rank format for broadcast, ensuring a symmetrically efficient cycle. We also propose an efficient variant, FedSRD-e, to reduce computational overhead. Experiments on 10 benchmarks show our framework significantly reduces communication costs by up to 90\\% while improving performance on heterogeneous client data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by WWW 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04601","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:29Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-26T02:29:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04058","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04058","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04058","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhodip","familyName":"Panda","name":"Panda, Subhodip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Varun M","familyName":"S","name":"S, Varun M","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shreyans","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Shreyans","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarthak Kumar","familyName":"Maharana","name":"Maharana, Sarthak Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prathosh A.","familyName":"P","name":"P, Prathosh A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unlearning in Diffusion models under Data Constraints: A Variational Inference Approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T06:39:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:49:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-10T13:26:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-11T02:35:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-11T16:41:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-12T01:58:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-23T04:07:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:28:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"For a responsible and safe deployment of diffusion models in various domains, regulating the generated outputs from these models is desirable because such models could generate undesired, violent, and obscene outputs. To tackle this problem, recent works use machine unlearning methodology to forget training data points containing these undesired features from pre-trained generative models. However, these methods proved to be ineffective in data-constrained settings where the whole training dataset is inaccessible. Thus, the principal objective of this work is to propose a machine unlearning methodology that can prevent the generation of outputs containing undesired features from a pre-trained diffusion model in such a data-constrained setting. Our proposed method, termed as Variational Diffusion Unlearning (VDU), is a computationally efficient method that only requires access to a subset of training data containing undesired features. Our approach is inspired by the variational inference framework with the objective of minimizing a loss function consisting of two terms: plasticity inducer and stability regularizer. Plasticity inducer reduces the log-likelihood of the undesired training data points, while the stability regularizer, essential for preventing loss of image generation quality, regularizes the model in parameter space. We validate the effectiveness of our method through comprehensive experiments for both class unlearning and feature unlearning. For class unlearning, we unlearn some user-identified classes from MNIST, CIFAR-10, and tinyImageNet datasets from a pre-trained unconditional denoising diffusion probabilistic model (DDPM). Similarly, for feature unlearning, we unlearn the generation of certain high-level features from a pre-trained Stable Diffusion model trained on LAION-5B dataset."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04058","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:09Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:16:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04476","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04476","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04476","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomas","familyName":"Figliolia","name":"Figliolia, Tomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Alonso","name":"Alonso, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishi","familyName":"Iyer","name":"Iyer, Rishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quentin","familyName":"Anthony","name":"Anthony, Quentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beren","familyName":"Millidge","name":"Millidge, Beren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compressed Convolutional Attention: Efficient Attention in a Compressed Latent Space"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T04:24:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:13:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-16T23:36:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-18T00:23:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-headed Attention's (MHA) quadratic compute and linearly growing KV-cache make long-context transformers expensive to train and serve. Prior works such as Grouped Query Attention (GQA) and Multi-Latent Attention (MLA) shrink the cache, speeding decode, but leave compute, which determines prefill and training speed, largely unchanged. We introduce Compressed Convolutional Attention (CCA), a novel attention method which down-projects queries, keys, and values and performs the entire attention operation inside the shared latent space. This simple design dramatically cuts parameters, KV-cache, and FLOPs all at once by the desired compression factor. Because CCA is orthogonal to head-sharing, we combine the two to form Compressed Convolutional Grouped Query Attention (CCGQA), which further tightens the compute-bandwidth Pareto frontier so that users can tune compression toward either FLOP or memory limits without sacrificing quality. Experiments show that CCGQA consistently outperforms both GQA and MLA at equal KV-cache compression on dense and MoE models. Additionally, we show that CCGQA outperforms all other attention methods on MoE models with half the KV-cache of GQA and MLA, achieving an 8x KV-cache compression with no drop in performance compared to standard MHA. CCA and CCGQA also dramatically reduce the FLOP cost of attention which leads to substantially faster training and prefill than existing methods. On H100 GPUs, our fused CCA/CCGQA kernel reduces prefill latency by about 1.7x at a sequence length of 16k relative to MHA, and accelerates backward by about 1.3x."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04476","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:27Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-18T02:49:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04282","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04282","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04282","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Idan"],"givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Kiu","name":"Kiu, Yu","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Organizational","affiliation":[],"name":"Lau","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Flexible and Efficient Spatio-Temporal Transformer for Sequential Visual Place Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T16:52:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:01:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-17T03:00:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-18T00:29:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sequential Visual Place Recognition (Seq-VPR) leverages transformers to capture spatio-temporal features effectively. In practice, a transformer-based Seq-VPR model should be flexible to the number of frames per sequence (seq- length), deliver fast inference, and have low memory usage to meet real-time constraints. However, existing approaches prioritize performance at the expense of flexibility and effi- ciency. To address this gap, we propose Adapt-STformer, a Seq-VPR method built around our novel Recurrent Deformable Transformer Encoder (Recurrent-DTE), which uses an iterative recurrent mechanism to fuse information from multiple sequen- tial frames. This design naturally supports variable seq-lengths, fast inference, and low memory usage. Experiments on the Nordland, Oxford, and NuScenes datasets show that Adapt- STformer boosts recall by up to 17% while reducing sequence extraction time by 36% and lowering memory usage by 35% relative to our best comparable baseline. Our code is released at https://ai4ce.github.io/Adapt-STFormer/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04282","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:41Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-18T02:49:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03813","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03813","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03813","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byungjun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Byungjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soobin","familyName":"Um","name":"Um, Soobin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jong Chul","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jong Chul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diverse Text-to-Image Generation via Contrastive Noise Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T13:51:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:35:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T04:37:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:21:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-16T13:07:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-17T02:16:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-image (T2I) diffusion models have demonstrated impressive performance in generating high-fidelity images, largely enabled by text-guided inference. However, this advantage often comes with a critical drawback: limited diversity, as outputs tend to collapse into similar modes under strong text guidance. Existing approaches typically optimize intermediate latents or text conditions during inference, but these methods deliver only modest gains or remain sensitive to hyperparameter tuning. In this work, we introduce Contrastive Noise Optimization, a simple yet effective method that addresses the diversity issue from a distinct perspective. Unlike prior techniques that adapt intermediate latents, our approach shapes the initial noise to promote diverse outputs. Specifically, we develop a contrastive loss defined in the Tweedie data space and optimize a batch of noise latents. Our contrastive optimization repels instances within the batch to maximize diversity while keeping them anchored to a reference sample to preserve fidelity. We further provide theoretical insights into the mechanism of this preprocessing to substantiate its effectiveness. Extensive experiments across multiple T2I backbones demonstrate that our approach achieves a superior quality-diversity Pareto frontier while remaining robust to hyperparameter choices."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03813","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:59:03Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:59:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-17T04:56:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03366","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03366","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03366","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshwardhan","familyName":"Fartale","name":"Fartale, Harshwardhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashish","familyName":"Kattamuri","name":"Kattamuri, Ashish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Raja","name":"Raja, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arpita","familyName":"Vats","name":"Vats, Arpita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ishita","familyName":"Prasad","name":"Prasad, Ishita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshata Kishore","familyName":"Moharir","name":"Moharir, Akshata Kishore","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Disentangling Recall and Reasoning in Transformer Models through Layer-wise Attention and Activation Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T04:13:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:03:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-13T08:24:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-16T00:33:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer-based language models excel at both recall (retrieving memorized facts) and reasoning (performing multi-step inference), but whether these abilities rely on distinct internal mechanisms remains unclear. Distinguishing recall from reasoning is crucial for predicting model generalization, designing targeted evaluations, and building safer interventions that affect one ability without disrupting the other.We approach this question through mechanistic interpretability, using controlled datasets of synthetic linguistic puzzles to probe transformer models at the layer, head, and neuron level. Our pipeline combines activation patching and structured ablations to causally measure component contributions to each task type. Across two model families (Qwen and LLaMA), we find that interventions on distinct layers and attention heads lead to selective impairments: disabling identified \"recall circuits\" reduces fact-retrieval accuracy by up to 15\\% while leaving reasoning intact, whereas disabling \"reasoning circuits\" reduces multi-step inference by a comparable margin. At the neuron level, we observe task-specific firing patterns, though these effects are less robust, consistent with neuronal polysemanticity.Our results provide the first causal evidence that recall and reasoning rely on separable but interacting circuits in transformer models. These findings advance mechanistic interpretability by linking circuit-level structure to functional specialization and demonstrate how controlled datasets and causal interventions can yield mechanistic insights into model cognition, informing safer deployment of large language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03366","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:48:10Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:48:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-16T02:07:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03666","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03666","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03666","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sichao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Sichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinsong","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yinsong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangyuan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Guangyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Haoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lifang","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Lifang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingliang","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Jingliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MonitorVLM:A Vision Language Framework for Safety Violation Detection in Mining Operations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T04:46:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:23:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-11T12:33:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-12T00:46:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Industrial accidents, particularly in high-risk domains such as surface and underground mining, are frequently caused by unsafe worker behaviors. Traditional manual inspection remains labor-intensive, error-prone, and insufficient for large-scale, dynamic environments, highlighting the urgent need for intelligent and automated safety monitoring. In this paper, we present MonitorVLM, a novel vision--language framework designed to detect safety violations directly from surveillance video streams. MonitorVLM introduces three key innovations: (1) a domain-specific violation dataset comprising 9,000 vision--question--answer (VQA) samples across 40 high-frequency mining regulations, enriched with augmentation and auxiliary detection cues; (2) a clause filter (CF) module that dynamically selects the Top-$K$ most relevant clauses, reducing inference latency by 13.56\\% while maintaining accuracy; and (3) a behavior magnifier (BM) module that enhances worker regions to improve fine-grained action recognition, yielding additional gains of 3.45% in precision and 8.62% in recall. Experimental results demonstrate that MonitorVLM significantly outperforms baseline vision--language models, achieving improvements of 22.01% in precision, 34.22\\% in recall, and 28.37% in F1 score over the 72B unfine-tuned baseline. A lightweight web-based interface further integrates MonitorVLM into practical workflows, enabling automatic violation reporting with video timestamping. This study highlights the potential of multimodal large models to enhance occupational safety monitoring in mining and beyond."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03666","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:55:27Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:55:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-12T02:25:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03929","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03929","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03929","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Campbell","name":"Campbell, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valentin","familyName":"De Bortoli","name":"De Bortoli, Valentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnaud","familyName":"Doucet","name":"Doucet, Arnaud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-Speculative Masked Diffusions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T20:16:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:42:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-06T10:45:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-09T00:38:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present self-speculative masked diffusions, a new class of masked diffusion generative models for discrete data that require significantly fewer function evaluations to generate samples. Standard masked diffusion models predict factorized logits over currently masked positions. A number of masked positions are then sampled, however, the factorization approximation means that sampling too many positions in one go leads to poor sample quality. As a result, many simulation steps and therefore neural network function evaluations are required to generate high-quality data. We reduce the computational burden by generating non-factorized predictions over masked positions. This is achieved by modifying the final transformer attention mask from non-causal to causal, enabling draft token generation and parallel validation via a novel, model-integrated speculative sampling mechanism. This results in a non-factorized predictive distribution over masked positions in a single forward pass. We apply our method to GPT2 scale text modelling and protein sequence generation, finding that we can achieve a ~2x reduction in the required number of network forward passes relative to standard masked diffusion models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages, 7 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03929","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:02:00Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:02:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-09T02:09:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04284","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04284","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04284","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunghwei","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Yunghwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Kaiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weizhi","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Weizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Doctor-R1: Mastering Clinical Inquiry with Experiential Agentic Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T16:54:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:01:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-06T06:28:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-09T01:24:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-01T08:43:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:05:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The professionalism of a human doctor in outpatient service depends on two core abilities: the ability to make accurate medical decisions and the medical consultation skill to conduct strategic, empathetic patient inquiry. Existing Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable accuracy on medical decision-making benchmarks. However, they often lack the ability to conduct the strategic and empathetic consultation, which is essential for real-world clinical scenarios. To address this gap, we propose Doctor-R1, an AI doctor agent trained to master both of the capabilities by ask high-yield questions and conduct strategic multi-turn inquiry to guide decision-making. Our framework introduces three key components: a multi-agent interactive environment, a two-tiered reward architecture that separately optimizes clinical decision-making and communicative inquiry skills, and an experience repository to ground policy learning in high-quality prior trajectories. We evaluate Doctor-R1 on OpenAI's HealthBench and MAQuE, assessed across multi-facet metrics, such as communication quality, user experience, and task accuracy. Remarkably, Doctor-R1 surpasses state-of-the-art open-source specialized LLMs by a substantial margin with higher parameter efficiency and outperforms powerful proprietary models. Furthermore, the human expert evaluations show that Doctor-R1 achieves superior clinical capability and patient-centric performance, demonstrating the effectiveness of the framework."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04284","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04080","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04080","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04080","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixin","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian-Wen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qian-Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunping","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chunping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PoLi-RL: A Point-to-List Reinforcement Learning Framework for Conditional Semantic Textual Similarity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T07:57:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:51:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-19T05:11:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-20T01:24:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-28T14:59:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:43:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Conditional Semantic Textual Similarity (C-STS) measures the semantic proximity between text segments under a specific condition, thereby overcoming the ambiguity inherent in traditional STS. However, existing methods are largely confined to discriminative models, failing to fully leverage recent breakthroughs in the NLP community involving Large Language Models (LLMs) and Reinforcement Learning (RL). RL is a particularly well-suited paradigm for this task, as it can directly optimize the non-differentiable Spearman ranking metric and guide the reasoning process required by C-STS. Nevertheless, we find that naively applying listwise RL fails to produce meaningful improvements, as the model struggles with complex, coarse-grained reward signals, leading to optimization difficulties. To address this challenge, we introduce PoLi-RL, a novel Point-to-List Reinforcement Learning framework. PoLi-RL employs a two-stage curriculum: it first trains the model with a simple pointwise reward to establish fundamental scoring capabilities, then transitions to a hybrid reward that combines pointwise, pairwise, and listwise objectives to refine the model's ability to discern subtle semantic distinctions. Crucially, we propose an innovative Parallel Slice Ranking Reward (PSRR) mechanism that computes ranking rewards in parallel slices, where each slice consists of completions with the same index from different samples. This provides a precise, differentiated learning signal for each individual completion, enabling granular credit assignment and effective optimization. On the official C-STS benchmark, PoLi-RL achieves a Spearman correlation coefficient of 48.18, establishing a new SOTA for the cross-encoder architecture. As the first work to successfully apply RL to C-STS, our study introduces a powerful paradigm for aligning LLMs for complex, ranking-based conditional judgment tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04080","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:42Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04373","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04373","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04373","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hadi","familyName":"Nekoei","name":"Nekoei, Hadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aman","familyName":"Jaiswal","name":"Jaiswal, Aman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrice","familyName":"Bechard","name":"Bechard, Patrice","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oleh","familyName":"Shliazhko","name":"Shliazhko, Oleh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Orlando Marquez","familyName":"Ayala","name":"Ayala, Orlando Marquez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathieu","familyName":"Reymond","name":"Reymond, Mathieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Massimo","familyName":"Caccia","name":"Caccia, Massimo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Drouin","name":"Drouin, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarath","familyName":"Chandar","name":"Chandar, Sarath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Lacoste","name":"Lacoste, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"JEF-Hinter: Leveraging Offline Knowledge for Improving Web Agents Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T21:34:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:07:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-20T19:34:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:04:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) agents perform well in sequential decision-making tasks, but improving them on unfamiliar domains often requires costly online interactions or fine-tuning on large expert datasets. These strategies are impractical for closed-source models and expensive for open-source ones, with risks of catastrophic forgetting. Offline trajectories offer reusable knowledge, yet demonstration-based methods struggle because raw traces are long, noisy, and tied to specific tasks. We present Just-in-time Episodic Feedback Hinter (JEF-Hinter), an agentic system that distills offline traces into compact, context-aware hints. A zooming mechanism highlights decisive steps in long trajectories, capturing both strategies and pitfalls. Unlike prior methods, JEF-Hinter leverages both successful and failed trajectories, extracting guidance even when only failure data is available, while supporting parallelized hint generation and benchmark-independent prompting. At inference, a retriever selects relevant hints for the current state, providing targeted guidance with transparency and traceability. Experiments on MiniWoB++, WorkArena-L1, and WebArena-Lite show that JEF-Hinter consistently outperforms strong baselines, including human- and document-based hints."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04373","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:55Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03817","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03817","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03817","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Becker","name":"Becker, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niklas","familyName":"Freymuth","name":"Freymuth, Niklas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Serge","familyName":"Thilges","name":"Thilges, Serge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian","familyName":"Otto","name":"Otto, Fabian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gerhard","familyName":"Neumann","name":"Neumann, Gerhard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TROLL: Trust Regions improve Reinforcement Learning for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T14:14:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:35:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-31T13:36:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T01:41:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-23T18:54:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-24T02:45:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning (RL) with PPO-like clip objectives has become the standard choice for reward-based fine-tuning of large language models (LLMs). Although recent work has explored improved estimators of advantages and normalization, the clipping mechanism itself has remained untouched. Originally introduced as a proxy for principled KL-based trust regions, clipping is a crude approximation that often causes unstable updates and suboptimal performance. We replace the clip objective with a novel discrete differentiable trust region projection, which provides principled token-level KL constraints. The projection operates on a sparse subset of the model's most important token logits to balance computational cost and projection effectiveness. Our approach, Trust Region Optimization for Large Language models (TROLL), serves as a direct replacement for PPO-like clipping during training and does not alter the model's inference behavior. Across mathematical reasoning and code generation tasks, model families, as well as advantage-estimation methods, TROLL consistently outperforms PPO-like clipping in terms of training speed, stability, and final success rates."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03817","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:59:09Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:59:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03346","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03346","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03346","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Chiesa","name":"Chiesa, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gerald Q.","familyName":"Maguire","name":"Maguire, Gerald Q.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dejan","familyName":"Kostic","name":"Kostic, Dejan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"KVComm: Enabling Efficient LLM Communication through Selective KV Sharing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T16:01:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:03:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-12T09:38:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-13T01:38:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-22T15:15:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:52:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in multi-agent systems, where effective inter-model communication is crucial. Existing communication protocols either rely on natural language, incurring high inference costs and information loss, or on hidden states, which suffer from information concentration bias and inefficiency. To address these limitations, we propose KVComm, a novel communication framework that enables efficient communication between LLMs through selective sharing of KV pairs. KVComm leverages the rich information encoded in the KV pairs while avoiding the pitfalls of hidden states. We introduce a KV layer-wise selection strategy based on attention importance scores with a Gaussian prior to identify the most informative KV pairs for communication. Extensive experiments across diverse tasks and model pairs demonstrate that KVComm achieves comparable performance to the upper-bound method, which directly merges inputs to one model without any communication, while transmitting as few as 30\\% of layers' KV pairs. Our study highlights the potential of KV pairs as an effective medium for inter-LLM communication, paving the way for scalable and efficient multi-agent systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03346","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:47:41Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:47:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03574","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03574","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03574","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehmet Onurcan","familyName":"Kaya","name":"Kaya, Mehmet Onurcan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Desmond","familyName":"Elliott","name":"Elliott, Desmond","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dim P.","familyName":"Papadopoulos","name":"Papadopoulos, Dim P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Test-Time Scaling for Small Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T23:49:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:14:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-16T15:56:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-17T02:42:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Small Vision-Language Models (VLMs) provide a computationally efficient alternative to larger models, at the cost of weaker generalization abilities and downstream task performance. These shortcomings could be addressed by test-time scaling techniques, but existing methods are typically computationally demanding, contradicting the resource-efficient design goals of small models. To address these limitations, we propose two novel and efficient test-time scaling strategies that leverage the model-internal features rather than external supervision: (i) Test-Time Augmentation (TTAug), which generates multiple augmented inputs and aggregates outputs at the token level without parameter updates, and (ii) Test-Time Adaptation (TTAdapt), which adapts model parameters during inference using consensus-based pseudolabels from TTAug. Through extensive experiments across nine benchmarks, we demonstrate consistent performance improvements while maintaining computational efficiency suitable for resource-constrained environments. The generality of our approach is demonstrated both within models at different scales and across different VLMs without additional tuning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026. Project Page: https://monurcan.github.io/efficient_test_time_scaling"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03574","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:53:13Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:53:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-17T04:46:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04787","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04787","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04787","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zifan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaitao","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Kaitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guosheng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Guosheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ding","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Ding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyao","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Junyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohua","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaohua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongsheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Dongsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cairong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Cairong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Trade in Minutes! Rationality-Driven Agentic System for Quantitative Financial Trading"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T13:08:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:32:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-09T07:01:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T02:32:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) and agentic systems have shown exceptional decision-making capabilities, revealing significant potential for autonomic finance. Current financial trading agents predominantly simulate anthropomorphic roles that inadvertently introduce emotional biases and rely on peripheral information, while being constrained by the necessity for continuous inference during deployment. In this paper, we pioneer the harmonization of strategic depth in agents with the mechanical rationality essential for quantitative trading. Consequently, we present TiMi (Trade in Minutes), a rationality-driven multi-agent system that architecturally decouples strategy development from minute-level deployment. TiMi leverages specialized LLM capabilities of semantic analysis, code programming, and mathematical reasoning within a comprehensive policy-optimization-deployment chain. Specifically, we propose a two-tier analytical paradigm from macro patterns to micro customization, layered programming design for trading bot implementation, and closed-loop optimization driven by mathematical reflection. Extensive evaluations across 200+ trading pairs in stock and cryptocurrency markets empirically validate the efficacy of TiMi in stable profitability, action efficiency, and risk control under volatile market dynamics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04787","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:23:04Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:23:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-10T05:21:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03999","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03999","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03999","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Yeh","name":"Yeh, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jwala","familyName":"Dhamala","name":"Dhamala, Jwala","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ousmane","familyName":"Dia","name":"Dia, Ousmane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sharon","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sharon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LH-Deception: Simulating and Understanding LLM Deceptive Behaviors in Long-Horizon Interactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T02:18:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T15:30:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:58:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-05T12:03:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:43:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deception is a pervasive feature of human communication and an emerging concern in large language models (LLMs). While recent studies document instances of LLM deception, most evaluations remain confined to single-turn prompts and fail to capture the long-horizon interactions in which deceptive strategies typically unfold. We introduce a new simulation framework, LH-Deception, for a systematic, empirical quantification of deception in LLMs under extended sequences of interdependent tasks and dynamic contextual pressures. LH-Deception is designed as a multi-agent system: a performer agent tasked with completing tasks and a supervisor agent that evaluates progress, provides feedback, and maintains evolving states of trust. An independent deception auditor then reviews full trajectories to identify when and how deception occurs. We conduct extensive experiments across 11 frontier models, spanning both closed-source and open-source systems, and find that deception is model-dependent, increases with event pressure, and consistently erodes supervisor trust. Qualitative analyses further reveal emergent, long-horizon phenomena, such as ``chains of deception\", which are invisible to static, single-turn evaluations. Our findings provide a foundation for evaluating future LLMs in real-world, trust-sensitive contexts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03999","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:42Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-06T03:21:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04347","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04347","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04347","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anindya Sundar","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Anindya Sundar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangjie","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kangjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Monowar","familyName":"Bhuyan","name":"Bhuyan, Monowar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unmasking Backdoors: An Explainable Defense via Gradient-Attention Anomaly Scoring for Pre-trained Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T20:15:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:05:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T11:17:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:45:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pre-trained language models have achieved remarkable success across a wide range of natural language processing (NLP) tasks, particularly when fine-tuned on large, domain-relevant datasets. However, they remain vulnerable to backdoor attacks, where adversaries embed malicious behaviors using trigger patterns in the training data. These triggers remain dormant during normal usage, but, when activated, can cause targeted misclassifications. In this work, we investigate the internal behavior of backdoored pre-trained encoder-based language models, focusing on the consistent shift in attention and gradient attribution when processing poisoned inputs; where the trigger token dominates both attention and gradient signals, overriding the surrounding context. We propose an inference-time defense that constructs anomaly scores by combining token-level attention and gradient information. Extensive experiments on text classification tasks across diverse backdoor attack scenarios demonstrate that our method significantly reduces attack success rates compared to existing baselines. Furthermore, we provide an interpretability-driven analysis of the scoring mechanism, shedding light on trigger localization and the robustness of the proposed defense."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages total (9 pages main text + 6 pages appendix + references), 16 figures. Preprint version; the final camera-ready version may differ. Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04347","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:17Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:25:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03267","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03267","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03267","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianglong","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Xianglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengzhu","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Chengzhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiteng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaicheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Kaicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotong","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Haotong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruobing","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Ruobing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingwu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xingwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yulun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PT$^2$-LLM: Post-Training Ternarization for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T03:01:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T05:42:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:28:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown impressive capabilities across diverse tasks, but their large memory and compute demands hinder deployment. Ternarization has gained attention as a promising compression technique, delivering substantial size reduction and high computational efficiency. However, its potential in the post-training quantization (PTQ) setting remains underexplored, due to the challenge of training-free parameter optimization and the quantization difficulty posed by outliers and dispersed weights. To address these issues, we propose PT$^2$-LLM, a post-training ternarization framework tailored for LLMs. At its core is an Asymmetric Ternary Quantizer equipped with a two-stage refinement pipeline: (1) Iterative Ternary Fitting (ITF), which alternates between optimal ternary grid construction and flexible rounding to minimize quantization error, and (2) Activation-aware Grid Alignment (AGA), which further refines the ternary grid to better match full-precision outputs. In addition, we propose a plug-and-play Structural Similarity-based Reordering (SSR) strategy that leverages inter-column structural similarity to ease quantization and mitigate outlier effects, further enhancing overall performance. Extensive experiments demonstrate that PT$^2$-LLM delivers competitive performance against state-of-the-art (SOTA) 2-bit PTQ methods with lower memory cost, while also accelerating both prefill and decoding to achieve end-to-end speedup. The code and models will be available at https://github.com/XIANGLONGYAN/PT2-LLM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03267","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:25:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03544","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03544","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03544","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuji","familyName":"Takubo","name":"Takubo, Yuji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniele","familyName":"Gammelli","name":"Gammelli, Daniele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Pavone","name":"Pavone, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone","familyName":"D'Amico","name":"D'Amico, Simone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agile Tradespace Exploration for Space Rendezvous Mission Design via Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T22:28:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:13:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-11T22:27:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:57:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spacecraft rendezvous enables on-orbit servicing, debris removal, and crewed docking, forming the foundation for a scalable space economy. Designing such missions requires rapid exploration of the tradespace between control cost and flight time across multiple candidate targets. However, multi-objective optimization in this setting is challenging, as the underlying constraints are often nonconvex, and mission designers must balance accuracy (e.g., solving the full problem) with efficiency (e.g., convex relaxations), slowing iteration and limiting design agility. To address these challenges, this paper proposes an AI-powered framework that enables agile and generalized rendezvous mission design. Given the orbital information of the target spacecraft, boundary conditions of the servicer, and a range of flight times, a transformer model generates a set of near-Pareto optimal trajectories across varying flight times in a single parallelized inference step, thereby enabling rapid mission trade studies. The model is further extended to accommodate variable flight times and perturbed orbital dynamics, supporting realistic multi-objective trade-offs. Validation on chance-constrained rendezvous problems in Earth orbits with passive safety constraints demonstrates that the model generalizes across both flight times and dynamics, consistently providing high-quality initial guesses that converge to superior solutions in fewer iterations. Moreover, the framework efficiently approximates the Pareto front, achieving runtimes comparable to convex relaxation by exploiting parallelized inference. Together, these results position the proposed framework as a practical surrogate for nonconvex trajectory generation and mark an important step toward AI-driven trajectory design for accelerating preliminary mission planning in real-world rendezvous applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03544","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:52:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:52:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:26:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03289","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03289","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03289","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haocheng","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Haocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cynthia Xin","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Cynthia Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edward Hong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Edward Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Why mask diffusion does not work"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T12:07:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-23T09:36:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-24T01:30:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The main advantages of diffusion language models over autoregressive (AR) models lie in their ability to support parallel generation and bidirectional attention, enabling a more controllable generation process. In recent years, open-source mask diffusion language models have emerged, most of which are based on a variant known as absorbing diffusion. However, this paper demonstrates why mask diffusion faces inherent difficulties in achieving parallel generation and bidirectional attention. We also propose the most effective training and inference strategies for mask diffusion."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03289","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:17Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-24T02:56:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04146","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04146","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04146","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minseo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Minseo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Coleman","familyName":"Hooper","name":"Hooper, Coleman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Tomar","name":"Tomar, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenfeng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chenfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehrdad","familyName":"Farajtabar","name":"Farajtabar, Mehrdad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael W.","familyName":"Mahoney","name":"Mahoney, Michael W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kurt","familyName":"Keutzer","name":"Keutzer, Kurt","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir","familyName":"Gholami","name":"Gholami, Amir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:50:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:54:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-15T16:36:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-16T02:44:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved state-of-the-art performance on a broad range of Natural Language Processing (NLP) tasks, including document processing and code generation. Autoregressive Language Models (ARMs), which generate tokens sequentially conditioned on all previous tokens, have been the predominant paradigm for LLMs. While these models have achieved high accuracy across a range of downstream tasks, they exhibit low arithmetic intensity due to the inherent sequential dependency in next-token prediction. Recently, Diffusion Language Models (DLMs) have emerged as a promising alternative architecture. DLMs generate output tokens in parallel, mitigating the limitations of sequential decoding. However, the performance implications of DLMs relative to commonly deployed ARMs are not fully understood. In this work, we present a comprehensive study of the performance characteristics of ARMs and DLMs, combining theoretical analysis with empirical profiling to characterize the trade-offs between these approaches. We show that although DLMs can achieve higher arithmetic intensity than ARMs by leveraging parallelism across token positions, they fail to scale effectively with longer contexts. We then explore block-wise decoding for DLMs, which decouples arithmetic intensity from sequence length and enables better scaling to long contexts (similar to ARMs). We also examine batched inference and find that ARMs exhibit superior throughput as they benefit more from parallelism across sequences in the batch. Finally, we highlight opportunities for accelerating DLM inference, emphasizing that reducing the number of sampling steps is key for open-source DLMs to achieve lower latency relative to ARMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04146","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-16T04:50:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03786","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03786","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03786","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"T-Mai","familyName":"Bui","name":"Bui, T-Mai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fares","familyName":"Bougourzi","name":"Bougourzi, Fares","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fadi","familyName":"Dornaika","name":"Dornaika, Fadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vinh Truong","familyName":"Hoang","name":"Hoang, Vinh Truong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MambaCAFU: Hybrid Multi-Scale and Multi-Attention Model with Mamba-Based Fusion for Medical Image Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T11:25:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:32:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T23:43:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:16:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, deep learning has shown near-expert performance in segmenting complex medical tissues and tumors. However, existing models are often task-specific, with performance varying across modalities and anatomical regions. Balancing model complexity and performance remains challenging, particularly in clinical settings where both accuracy and efficiency are critical. To address these issues, we propose a hybrid segmentation architecture featuring a three-branch encoder that integrates CNNs, Transformers, and a Mamba-based Attention Fusion (MAF) mechanism to capture local, global, and long-range dependencies. A multi-scale attention-based CNN decoder reconstructs fine-grained segmentation maps while preserving contextual consistency. Additionally, a co-attention gate enhances feature selection by emphasizing relevant spatial and semantic information across scales during both encoding and decoding, improving feature interaction and cross-scale communication. Extensive experiments on multiple benchmark datasets show that our approach outperforms state-of-the-art methods in accuracy and generalization, while maintaining comparable computational complexity. By effectively balancing efficiency and effectiveness, our architecture offers a practical and scalable solution for diverse medical imaging tasks. Source code and trained models will be publicly released upon acceptance to support reproducibility and further research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03786","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:58:24Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:58:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-30T02:18:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03597","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03597","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03597","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sina","familyName":"Alemohammad","name":"Alemohammad, Sina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhangyang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhangyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard G.","familyName":"Baraniuk","name":"Baraniuk, Richard G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T01:20:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:17:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T13:29:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:48:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T22:13:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:11:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scaling generative AI models is bottlenecked by the scarcity of high-quality training data. The ease of synthesizing from a generative model suggests using (unverified) synthetic data to augment a limited corpus of real data for the purpose of fine-tuning in the hope of improving performance. Unfortunately, however, the resulting positive feedback loop leads to model autophagy disorder (MAD, aka model collapse) that results in a rapid degradation in sample quality and/or diversity. In this paper, we introduce Neon (for Negative Extrapolation frOm self-traiNing), a new learning method that turns the degradation from self-training into a powerful signal for self-improvement. Given a base model, Neon first fine-tunes it on its own self-synthesized data but then, counterintuitively, reverses its gradient updates to extrapolate away from the degraded weights. We prove that Neon works because typical inference samplers that favor high-probability regions create a predictable anti-alignment between the synthetic and real data population gradients, which negative extrapolation corrects to better align the model with the true data distribution. Neon is remarkably easy to implement via a simple post-hoc merge that requires no new real data, works effectively with as few as 1k synthetic samples, and typically uses less than 1% additional training compute. We demonstrate Neon's universality across a range of architectures (diffusion, flow matching, autoregressive, and inductive moment matching models) and datasets (ImageNet, CIFAR-10, and FFHQ). In particular, on ImageNet 256x256, Neon elevates the xAR-L model to a new state-of-the-art FID of 1.02 with only 0.36% additional training compute. Code is available at https://github.com/VITA-Group/Neon"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03597","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:53:47Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:53:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-15T02:22:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04550","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04550","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04550","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenwei","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Zhenwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"He","name":"He, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianfeng","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Xianfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanqing","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Hanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juanhui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Juanhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayuan","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Jiayuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhabrata","familyName":"Mukherjee","name":"Mukherjee, Subhabrata","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suhang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Suhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiliang","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jiliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benoit","familyName":"Dumoulin","name":"Dumoulin, Benoit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TRAJECT-Bench:A Trajectory-Aware Benchmark for Evaluating Agentic Tool Use"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T07:30:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:18:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T09:19:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:31:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM)-based agents increasingly rely on tool use to complete real-world tasks. While existing works evaluate the LLMs' tool use capability, they largely focus on the final answers yet overlook the detailed tool usage trajectory, i.e., whether tools are selected, parameterized, and ordered correctly. We introduce TRAJECT-Bench, a trajectory-aware benchmark to comprehensively evaluate LLMs' tool use capability through diverse tasks with fine-grained evaluation metrics. TRAJECT-Bench pairs high-fidelity, executable tools across practical domains with tasks grounded in production-style APIs, and synthesizes trajectories that vary in breadth (parallel calls) and depth (interdependent chains). Besides final accuracy, TRAJECT-Bench also reports trajectory-level diagnostics, including tool selection and argument correctness, and dependency/order satisfaction. Analyses reveal failure modes such as similar tool confusion and parameter-blind selection, and scaling behavior with tool diversity and trajectory length where the bottleneck of transiting from short to mid-length trajectories is revealed, offering actionable guidance for LLMs' tool use."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04550","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:15Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03298","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03298","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03298","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongqi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Dongqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjin","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Wenjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CAFL-L: Constraint-Aware Federated Learning with Lagrangian Dual Optimization for On-Device Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T22:07:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T15:06:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:55:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Constraint-Aware Federated Learning with Lagrangian Dual Optimization (CAFL-L), a principled extension of FedAvg that explicitly incorporates device-level resource constraints including energy, communication, memory, and thermal budgets. CAFL-L employs Lagrangian dual optimization to dynamically adapt training hyperparameters -- freezing depth, local steps, batch size, and communication compression -- while preserving training stability through token-budget preservation via gradient accumulation. Experiments on a character-level language model demonstrate that CAFL-L achieves superior constraint satisfaction compared to standard FedAvg (reducing memory usage by 20% and communication by 95%) while maintaining competitive validation performance, making it practical for deployment on resource-constrained edge devices."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by 39th NeurIPS - Constrained Optimization for Machine Learning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03298","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:32Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:17:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04764","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04764","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04764","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raha","familyName":"Askari","name":"Askari, Raha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sina","familyName":"Zarrieß","name":"Zarrieß, Sina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Özge","familyName":"Alacam","name":"Alacam, Özge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Judith","familyName":"Sieker","name":"Sieker, Judith","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are BabyLMs Deaf to Gricean Maxims? A Pragmatic Evaluation of Sample-efficient Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T12:38:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:31:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T09:14:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:35:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Implicit meanings are integral to human communication, making it essential for language models to be capable of identifying and interpreting them. Grice (1975) proposed a set of conversational maxims that guide cooperative dialogue, noting that speakers may deliberately violate these principles to express meanings beyond literal words, and that listeners, in turn, recognize such violations to draw pragmatic inferences.\n Building on Surian et al. (1996)'s study of children's sensitivity to violations of Gricean maxims, we introduce a novel benchmark to test whether language models pretrained on less than 10M and less than 100M tokens can distinguish maxim-adhering from maxim-violating utterances. We compare these BabyLMs across five maxims and situate their performance relative to children and a Large Language Model (LLM) pretrained on 3T tokens.\n We find that overall, models trained on less than 100M tokens outperform those trained on less than 10M, yet fall short of child-level and LLM competence. Our results suggest that modest data increases improve some aspects of pragmatic behavior, leading to finer-grained differentiation between pragmatic dimensions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for the BabyLM workshop in EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04764","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:22:29Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:22:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:21:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04738","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04738","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04738","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baher","familyName":"Mohammad","name":"Mohammad, Baher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Magauiya","familyName":"Zhussip","name":"Zhussip, Magauiya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stamatios","familyName":"Lefkimmiatis","name":"Lefkimmiatis, Stamatios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Speak, Edit, Repeat: High-Fidelity Voice Editing and Zero-Shot TTS with Cross-Attentive Mamba"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T12:11:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:30:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce MAVE (Mamba with Cross-Attention for Voice Editing and Synthesis), a novel autoregressive architecture for text-conditioned voice editing and high-fidelity text-to-speech (TTS) synthesis, built on a cross-attentive Mamba backbone. MAVE achieves state-of-the-art performance in speech editing and very competitive results in zero-shot TTS, while not being explicitly trained on the latter task, outperforming leading autoregressive and diffusion models on diverse, real-world audio. By integrating Mamba for efficient audio sequence modeling with cross-attention for precise text-acoustic alignment, MAVE enables context-aware voice editing with exceptional naturalness and speaker consistency. In pairwise human evaluations on a random 40-sample subset of the RealEdit benchmark (400 judgments), 57.2% of listeners rated MAVE - edited speech as perceptually equal to the original, while 24.8% prefered the original and 18.0% MAVE - demonstrating that in the majority of cases edits are indistinguishable from the source. MAVE compares favorably with VoiceCraft and FluentSpeech both on pairwise comparisons and standalone mean opinion score (MOS) evaluations. For zero-shot TTS, MAVE exceeds VoiceCraft in both speaker similarity and naturalness, without requiring multiple inference runs or post-processing. Remarkably, these quality gains come with a significantly lower memory cost and approximately the same latency: MAVE requires ~6x less memory than VoiceCraft during inference on utterances from the RealEdit database (mean duration: 6.21s, A100, FP16, batch size 1). Our results demonstrate that MAVE establishes a new standard for flexible, high-fidelity voice editing and synthesis through the synergistic integration of structured state-space modeling and cross-modal attention."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04738","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:21:50Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:21:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:21:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04725","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04725","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04725","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riku","familyName":"Anttila","name":"Anttila, Riku","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sub-Gaussian heat kernel estimates for reflected diffusion on inner uniform domains"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Functional Analysis (math.FA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Metric Geometry (math.MG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"31C25 35K08 31E05 46E36","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T11:45:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:29:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove that sub-Gaussian heat kernel estimates are inherited from a diffusion process on the ambient space to the reflected diffusion process on a subset which is an inner uniform domain."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, comments are welcome!"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04725","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:21:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:21:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:21:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04710","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04710","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04710","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zexin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changhua","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Changhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengyue","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Hengyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Quan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotian","familyName":"Si","name":"Si, Haotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianhui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jianhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaogang","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Gaogang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingjing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jingjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ViTs: Teaching Machines to See Time Series Anomalies Like Human Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T11:24:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:28:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Web service administrators must ensure the stability of multiple systems by promptly detecting anomalies in Key Performance Indicators (KPIs). Achieving the goal of \"train once, infer across scenarios\" remains a fundamental challenge for time series anomaly detection models. Beyond improving zero-shot generalization, such models must also flexibly handle sequences of varying lengths during inference, ranging from one hour to one week, without retraining. Conventional approaches rely on sliding-window encoding and self-supervised learning, which restrict inference to fixed-length inputs. Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable zero-shot capabilities across general domains. However, when applied to time series data, they face inherent limitations due to context length. To address this issue, we propose ViTs, a Vision-Language Model (VLM)-based framework that converts time series curves into visual representations. By rescaling time series images, temporal dependencies are preserved while maintaining a consistent input size, thereby enabling efficient processing of arbitrarily long sequences without context constraints. Training VLMs for this purpose introduces unique challenges, primarily due to the scarcity of aligned time series image-text data. To overcome this, we employ an evolutionary algorithm to automatically generate thousands of high-quality image-text pairs and design a three-stage training pipeline consisting of: (1) time series knowledge injection, (2) anomaly detection enhancement, and (3) anomaly reasoning refinement. Extensive experiments demonstrate that ViTs substantially enhance the ability of VLMs to understand and detect anomalies in time series data. All datasets and code will be publicly released at: https://anonymous.4open.science/r/ViTs-C484/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04710","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:21:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:21:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:21:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04668","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04668","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04668","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Habin","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Habin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeongseob","familyName":"Won","name":"Won, Yeongseob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juwon","familyName":"Seo","name":"Seo, Juwon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gyeong-Moon","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Gyeong-Moon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ConceptSplit: Decoupled Multi-Concept Personalization of Diffusion Models via Token-wise Adaptation and Attention Disentanglement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T10:22:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:25:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, multi-concept personalization for text-to-image (T2I) diffusion models to represent several subjects in an image has gained much more attention. The main challenge of this task is \"concept mixing\", where multiple learned concepts interfere or blend undesirably in the output image. To address this issue, in this paper, we present ConceptSplit, a novel framework to split the individual concepts through training and inference. Our framework comprises two key components. First, we introduce Token-wise Value Adaptation (ToVA), a merging-free training method that focuses exclusively on adapting the value projection in cross-attention. Based on our empirical analysis, we found that modifying the key projection, a common approach in existing methods, can disrupt the attention mechanism and lead to concept mixing. Second, we propose Latent Optimization for Disentangled Attention (LODA), which alleviates attention entanglement during inference by optimizing the input latent. Through extensive qualitative and quantitative experiments, we demonstrate that ConceptSplit achieves robust multi-concept personalization, mitigating unintended concept interference. Code is available at https://github.com/KU-VGI/ConceptSplit"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 13 figures, to be published in ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04668","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:20:06Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:20:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:20:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04644","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04644","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04644","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hirotsugu","familyName":"Kakugawa","name":"Kakugawa, Hirotsugu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sayaka","familyName":"Kamei","name":"Kamei, Sayaka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masahiro","familyName":"Shibata","name":"Shibata, Masahiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fukuhito","familyName":"Ooshita","name":"Ooshita, Fukuhito","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The R(1)W(1) Communication Model for Self-Stabilizing Distributed Algorithms"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:45:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:23:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Self-stabilization is a versatile methodology in the design of fault-tolerant distributed algorithms for transient faults. A self-stabilizing system automatically recovers from any kind and any finite number of transient faults. This property is specifically useful in modern distributed systems with a large number of components. In this paper, we propose a new communication and execution model named the R(1)W(1) model in which each process can read and write its own and neighbors' local variables in a single step. We propose self-stabilizing distributed algorithms in the R(1)W(1) model for the problems of maximal matching, minimal k-dominating set and maximal k-dependent set. Finally, we propose an example transformer, based on randomized distance-two local mutual exclusion, to simulate algorithms designed for the R(1)W(1) model in the synchronous message passing model with synchronized clocks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04644","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:31Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:19:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04605","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04605","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04605","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jingyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianlin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring the Power of Diffusion Large Language Models for Software Engineering: An Empirical Investigation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:13:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:21:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autoregressive Large Language Models (AR-LLMs) are widely used in software engineering (SE) but face limitations in processing code structure information and suffer from high inference latency. Diffusion LLMs (DLLMs) offer a promising alternative with global bidirectional encoding and decoupled generation steps. This work presents the first comprehensive evaluation of DLLMs across the software development lifecycle, including code generation, defect detection, and program repair. On a large-scale benchmark of 52,937 tasks, 7Bparameter DLLMs outperform AR-LLMs with a 30% average accuracy improvement achieving a 113% gain on cross-file repair, while maintaining superior efficiency and reduced latency. Our results establish DLLMs as a superior paradigm for SE tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04605","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:35Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:18:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04577","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04577","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04577","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juncheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Juncheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoqi","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Guoqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shujun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Language Model Based Text-to-Audio Generation: Anti-Causally Aligned Collaborative Residual Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:26:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While language models (LMs) paired with residual vector quantization (RVQ) tokenizers have shown promise in text-to-audio (T2A) generation, they still lag behind diffusion-based models by a non-trivial margin. We identify a critical dilemma underpinning this gap: incorporating more RVQ layers improves audio reconstruction fidelity but exceeds the generation capacity of conventional LMs. To address this, we first analyze RVQ dynamics and uncover two key limitations: 1) orthogonality of features across RVQ layers hinders effective LMs training, and 2) descending semantic richness in tokens from deeper RVQ layers exacerbates exposure bias during autoregressive decoding. Based on these insights, we propose Siren, a novel LM-based framework that employs multiple isolated transformers with causal conditioning and anti-causal alignment via reinforcement learning. Extensive experiments demonstrate that Siren outperforms both existing LM-based and diffusion-based T2A systems, achieving state-of-the-art results. By bridging the representational strengths of LMs with the fidelity demands of audio synthesis, our approach repositions LMs as competitive contenders against diffusion models in T2A tasks. Moreover, by aligning audio representations with linguistic structures, Siren facilitates a promising pathway toward unified multi-modal generation frameworks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04577","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:54Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:17:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04568","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04568","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04568","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naman","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Naman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shreeyash","familyName":"Gowaikar","name":"Gowaikar, Shreeyash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arun","familyName":"Iyer","name":"Iyer, Arun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kirankumar","familyName":"Shiragur","name":"Shiragur, Kirankumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramakrishna B","familyName":"Bairi","name":"Bairi, Ramakrishna B","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishikesh","familyName":"Maurya","name":"Maurya, Rishikesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ritabrata","familyName":"Maiti","name":"Maiti, Ritabrata","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sankarshan","familyName":"Damle","name":"Damle, Sankarshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shachee Mishra","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Shachee Mishra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"COSMIR: Chain Orchestrated Structured Memory for Iterative Reasoning over Long Context"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:10:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning over very long inputs remains difficult for large language models (LLMs). Common workarounds either shrink the input via retrieval (risking missed evidence), enlarge the context window (straining selectivity), or stage multiple agents to read in pieces. In staged pipelines (e.g., Chain of Agents, CoA), free-form summaries passed between agents can discard crucial details and amplify early mistakes. We introduce COSMIR (Chain Orchestrated Structured Memory for Iterative Reasoning), a chain-style framework that replaces ad hoc messages with a structured memory. A Planner agent first turns a user query into concrete, checkable sub-questions. worker agents process chunks via a fixed micro-cycle: Extract, Infer, Refine, writing all updates to the shared memory. A Manager agent then Synthesizes the final answer directly from the memory. This preserves step-wise read-then-reason benefits while changing both the communication medium (structured memory) and the worker procedure (fixed micro-cycle), yielding higher faithfulness, better long-range aggregation, and auditability. On long-context QA from the HELMET suite, COSMIR reduces propagation-stage information loss and improves accuracy over a CoA baseline."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04568","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:41Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:17:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04542","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04542","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04542","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wolfgang","familyName":"Lehrach","name":"Lehrach, Wolfgang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Hennes","name":"Hennes, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miguel","familyName":"Lazaro-Gredilla","name":"Lazaro-Gredilla, Miguel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinghua","familyName":"Lou","name":"Lou, Xinghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carter","familyName":"Wendelken","name":"Wendelken, Carter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antoine","familyName":"Dedieu","name":"Dedieu, Antoine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jordi","familyName":"Grau-Moya","name":"Grau-Moya, Jordi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc","familyName":"Lanctot","name":"Lanctot, Marc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atil","familyName":"Iscen","name":"Iscen, Atil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Schultz","name":"Schultz, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcus","familyName":"Chiam","name":"Chiam, Marcus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ian","familyName":"Gemp","name":"Gemp, Ian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Piotr","familyName":"Zielinski","name":"Zielinski, Piotr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satinder","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Satinder","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin P.","familyName":"Murphy","name":"Murphy, Kevin P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Code World Models for General Game Playing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T07:16:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:17:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) reasoning abilities are increasingly being applied to classical board and card games, but the dominant approach -- involving prompting for direct move generation -- has significant drawbacks. It relies on the model's implicit fragile pattern-matching capabilities, leading to frequent illegal moves and strategically shallow play. Here we introduce an alternative approach: We use the LLM to translate natural language rules and game trajectories into a formal, executable world model represented as Python code. This generated model -- comprising functions for state transition, legal move enumeration, and termination checks -- serves as a verifiable simulation engine for high-performance planning algorithms like Monte Carlo tree search (MCTS). In addition, we prompt the LLM to generate heuristic value functions (to make MCTS more efficient), and inference functions (to estimate hidden states in imperfect information games). Our method offers three distinct advantages compared to directly using the LLM as a policy: (1) Verifiability: The generated CWM serves as a formal specification of the game's rules, allowing planners to algorithmically enumerate valid actions and avoid illegal moves, contingent on the correctness of the synthesized model; (2) Strategic Depth: We combine LLM semantic understanding with the deep search power of classical planners; and (3) Generalization: We direct the LLM to focus on the meta-task of data-to-code translation, enabling it to adapt to new games more easily. We evaluate our agent on 10 different games, of which 4 are novel and created for this paper. 5 of the games are fully observed (perfect information), and 5 are partially observed (imperfect information). We find that our method outperforms or matches Gemini 2.5 Pro in 9 out of the 10 considered games."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04542","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:03Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:17:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04463","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04463","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04463","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takashi","familyName":"Maekaku","name":"Maekaku, Takashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keita","familyName":"Goto","name":"Goto, Keita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinchuan","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Jinchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusuke","familyName":"Shinohara","name":"Shinohara, Yusuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shinji","familyName":"Watanabe","name":"Watanabe, Shinji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Self-Supervised Speech Models via Text-Based LLMS"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T03:25:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:13:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Self-Supervised Learning (SSL) has gained traction for its ability to learn rich representations with low labeling costs, applicable across diverse downstream tasks. However, assessing the downstream-task performance remains challenging due to the cost of extra training and evaluation. Existing methods for task-agnostic evaluation also require extra training or hyperparameter tuning. We propose a novel evaluation metric using large language models (LLMs). By inputting discrete token sequences and minimal domain cues derived from SSL models into LLMs, we obtain the mean log-likelihood; these cues guide in-context learning, rendering the score more reliable without extra training or hyperparameter tuning. Experimental results show a correlation between LLM-based scores and automatic speech recognition task. Additionally, our findings reveal that LLMs not only functions as an SSL evaluation tools but also provides inference-time embeddings that are useful for speaker verification task."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ASRU 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04463","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:15:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04450","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04450","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04450","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiyuan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Qiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yicong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yicong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotian","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Haotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyao","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Xinyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pheng-Ann","familyName":"Heng","name":"Heng, Pheng-Ann","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Ermon","name":"Ermon, Stefano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angela","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Angela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"REAR: Rethinking Visual Autoregressive Models via Generator-Tokenizer Consistency Regularization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T02:48:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:12:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visual autoregressive (AR) generation offers a promising path toward unifying vision and language models, yet its performance remains suboptimal against diffusion models. Prior work often attributes this gap to tokenizer limitations and rasterization ordering. In this work, we identify a core bottleneck from the perspective of generator-tokenizer inconsistency, i.e., the AR-generated tokens may not be well-decoded by the tokenizer. To address this, we propose reAR, a simple training strategy introducing a token-wise regularization objective: when predicting the next token, the causal transformer is also trained to recover the visual embedding of the current token and predict the embedding of the target token under a noisy context. It requires no changes to the tokenizer, generation order, inference pipeline, or external models. Despite its simplicity, reAR substantially improves performance. On ImageNet, it reduces gFID from 3.02 to 1.86 and improves IS to 316.9 using a standard rasterization-based tokenizer. When applied to advanced tokenizers, it achieves a gFID of 1.42 with only 177M parameters, matching the performance with larger state-of-the-art diffusion models (675M)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 23 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04450","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:14:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:14:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:14:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04354","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04354","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04354","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Apurva","familyName":"Badithela","name":"Badithela, Apurva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Snyder","name":"Snyder, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lihan","familyName":"Zha","name":"Zha, Lihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph","familyName":"Mikhail","name":"Mikhail, Joseph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"O'Kelly","name":"O'Kelly, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anushri","familyName":"Dixit","name":"Dixit, Anushri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anirudha","familyName":"Majumdar","name":"Majumdar, Anirudha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reliable and Scalable Robot Policy Evaluation with Imperfect Simulators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T20:37:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:06:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Rapid progress in imitation learning, foundation models, and large-scale datasets has led to robot manipulation policies that generalize to a wide-range of tasks and environments. However, rigorous evaluation of these policies remains a challenge. Typically in practice, robot policies are often evaluated on a small number of hardware trials without any statistical assurances. We present SureSim, a framework to augment large-scale simulation with relatively small-scale real-world testing to provide reliable inferences on the real-world performance of a policy. Our key idea is to formalize the problem of combining real and simulation evaluations as a prediction-powered inference problem, in which a small number of paired real and simulation evaluations are used to rectify bias in large-scale simulation. We then leverage non-asymptotic mean estimation algorithms to provide confidence intervals on mean policy performance. Using physics-based simulation, we evaluate both diffusion policy and multi-task fine-tuned \\(π_0\\) on a joint distribution of objects and initial conditions, and find that our approach saves over \\(20-25\\%\\) of hardware evaluation effort to achieve similar bounds on policy performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04354","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:28Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:12:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04286","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04286","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04286","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshil","familyName":"Vejendla","name":"Vejendla, Harshil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SliceMoE: Routing Embedding Slices Instead of Tokens for Fine-Grained and Balanced Transformer Scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T16:57:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:02:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layers scale transformers by routing tokens to a sparse subset of feed-forward experts. Token-level routing, however, assigns an entire semantic spectrum to each expert, creating capacity bottlenecks, load-balancing pathologies, and limited specialization. We introduce SliceMoE, an architecture that routes contiguous slices of a token's hidden vector. A d-dimensional embedding is partitioned into S slices, and for each slice, a lightweight shared router predicts the top-k experts. Experts operate on their assigned slices independently, and outputs are reassembled, maintaining per-token FLOP efficiency. Because slices from different tokens interleave within an expert, utilization is naturally smoother. We propose a slice-level capacity loss, cross-slice dropout, and efficient fused batched GEMM kernels. Experiments on WikiText-103 language modeling, WMT En-De translation, and three text-classification datasets show SliceMoE attains up to 1.7x faster inference than dense baselines, 12 to 18 percent lower perplexity than parameter-matched token-MoE, and improved expert balance, with interpretable expertise over syntactic versus semantic subspaces."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Main, 8 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04286","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:47Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:10:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04246","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04246","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04246","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huiwon","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Huiwon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sihyun","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Sihyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heeseung","familyName":"Kwon","name":"Kwon, Heeseung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hojin","familyName":"Jeon","name":"Jeon, Hojin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Younggyo","familyName":"Seo","name":"Seo, Younggyo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinwoo","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Jinwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ContextVLA: Vision-Language-Action Model with Amortized Multi-Frame Context"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T15:29:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:59:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Leveraging temporal context is crucial for success in partially observable robotic tasks. However, prior work in behavior cloning has demonstrated inconsistent performance gains when using multi-frame observations. In this paper, we introduce ContextVLA, a policy model that robustly improves robotic task performance by effectively leveraging multi-frame observations. Our approach is motivated by the key observation that Vision-Language-Action models (VLA), i.e., policy models built upon a Vision-Language Model (VLM), more effectively utilize multi-frame observations for action generation. This suggests that VLMs' inherent temporal understanding capability enables them to extract more meaningful context from multi-frame observations. However, the high dimensionality of video inputs introduces significant computational overhead, making VLA training and inference inefficient. To address this, ContextVLA compresses past observations into a single context token, allowing the policy to efficiently leverage temporal context for action generation. Our experiments show that ContextVLA consistently improves over single-frame VLAs and achieves the benefits of full multi-frame training but with reduced training and inference times."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://huiwon-jang.github.io/contextvla"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04246","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:09:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:09:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:09:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04162","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04162","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04162","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aviv","familyName":"Navon","name":"Navon, Aviv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aviv","familyName":"Shamsian","name":"Shamsian, Aviv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neta","familyName":"Glazer","name":"Glazer, Neta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yael","familyName":"Segal-Feldman","name":"Segal-Feldman, Yael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gill","familyName":"Hetz","name":"Hetz, Gill","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph","familyName":"Keshet","name":"Keshet, Joseph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan","familyName":"Fetaya","name":"Fetaya, Ethan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Drax: Speech Recognition with Discrete Flow Matching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T11:38:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:55:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion and flow-based non-autoregressive (NAR) models have shown strong promise in large language modeling, however, their potential for automatic speech recognition (ASR) remains largely unexplored. We propose Drax, a discrete flow matching framework for ASR that enables efficient parallel decoding. To better align training with inference, we construct an audio-conditioned probability path that guides the model through trajectories resembling likely intermediate inference errors, rather than direct random noise to target transitions. Our theoretical analysis links the generalization gap to divergences between training and inference occupancies, controlled by cumulative velocity errors, thereby motivating our design choice. Empirical evaluation demonstrates that our approach attains recognition accuracy on par with state-of-the-art speech models while offering improved accuracy-efficiency trade-offs, highlighting discrete flow matching as a promising direction for advancing NAR ASR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04162","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:45Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:07:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04153","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04153","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04153","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoqi","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Haoqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ObCLIP: Oblivious CLoud-Device Hybrid Image Generation with Privacy Preservation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T11:09:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:54:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion Models have gained significant popularity due to their remarkable capabilities in image generation, albeit at the cost of intensive computation requirement. Meanwhile, despite their widespread deployment in inference services such as Midjourney, concerns about the potential leakage of sensitive information in uploaded user prompts have arisen. Existing solutions either lack rigorous privacy guarantees or fail to strike an effective balance between utility and efficiency. To bridge this gap, we propose ObCLIP, a plug-and-play safeguard that enables oblivious cloud-device hybrid generation. By oblivious, each input prompt is transformed into a set of semantically similar candidate prompts that differ only in sensitive attributes (e.g., gender, ethnicity). The cloud server processes all candidate prompts without knowing which one is the real one, thus preventing any prompt leakage. To mitigate server cost, only a small portion of denoising steps is performed upon the large cloud model. The intermediate latents are then sent back to the client, which selects the targeted latent and completes the remaining denoising using a small device model. Additionally, we analyze and incorporate several cache-based accelerations that leverage temporal and batch redundancy, effectively reducing computation cost with minimal utility degradation. Extensive experiments across multiple datasets demonstrate that ObCLIP provides rigorous privacy and comparable utility to cloud models with slightly increased server cost."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04153","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:32Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:07:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04147","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04147","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04147","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifeng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziang","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Ziang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biqing","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Biqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanlin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hanlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self Speculative Decoding for Diffusion Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:52:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:54:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion-based Large Language Models (dLLMs) have emerged as a competitive alternative to autoregressive models, offering unique advantages through bidirectional attention and parallel generation paradigms. However, the generation results of current parallel decoding methods deviate from stepwise decoding, introducing potential performance degradation, which limits their practical deployment. To address this problem, we propose \\textbf{S}elf \\textbf{S}peculative \\textbf{D}ecoding (SSD), a lossless inference acceleration method that leverages the dLLM itself as both speculative decoding drafter and verifier without auxiliary modules. SSD introduces a self-drafting mechanism where the model generates predictions for multiple positions, then verifies them through hierarchical verification trees in a single forward pass. Unlike traditional speculative decoding that requires separate draft models, SSD eliminates model redundancy and memory overhead by exploiting the dLLM's inherent parallel prediction capability for multiple positions. This self-speculative approach allows the model to progressively verify and accept multiple tokens in a single forward pass. Our experiments demonstrate that SSD achieves up to 3.46$\\times$ speedup while keeping the output identical to stepwise decoding on open source models such as LLaDA and Dream. Code will be made publicly available on GitHub."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04147","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:23Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:07:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04136","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04136","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04136","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Umberto","familyName":"Cappellazzo","name":"Cappellazzo, Umberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minsu","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Minsu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pingchuan","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Pingchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honglie","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Honglie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xubo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xubo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stavros","familyName":"Petridis","name":"Petridis, Stavros","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maja","familyName":"Pantic","name":"Pantic, Maja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MoME: Mixture of Matryoshka Experts for Audio-Visual Speech Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:34:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:54:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have recently shown strong potential in audio-visual speech recognition (AVSR), but their high computational demands and sensitivity to token granularity limit their practicality in resource-constrained settings. Token compression methods can reduce inference cost, but they require fixing a compression rate in advance and produce a single fixed-length output, offering no flexibility to balance information density and efficiency at inference time. Matryoshka representation learning (MRL) addresses this by enabling a single model to operate across multiple token granularities, allowing compression rates to be adjusted dynamically. However, current MRL-based methods treat each scale independently during training, limiting cross-scale generalization, robustness at high compression, and interpretability. To overcome these limitations, we propose MoME (Mixture of Matryoshka Experts), a novel framework that integrates sparse Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) into MRL-based LLMs for AVSR. MoME augments a frozen LLM with top-k routed and shared experts, allowing dynamic capacity allocation across scales and modalities. A shared router promotes consistent expert activation across granularities, enabling compressed sequences to benefit from representations learned at lower compression. Experiments on LRS2 and LRS3 demonstrate that MoME achieves state-of-the-art performance across AVSR, ASR, and VSR tasks, while requiring significantly fewer parameters and maintaining robustness under noise. MoME unifies the adaptability of MRL with the efficiency of MoE, offering a scalable and interpretable solution for resource-aware speech recognition."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04136","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:06Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:07:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04125","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04125","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04125","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghyun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Seunghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tae-Kyun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Tae-Kyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Joint Learning of Pose Regression and Denoising Diffusion with Score Scaling Sampling for Category-level 6D Pose Estimation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T09:58:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:53:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Latest diffusion models have shown promising results in category-level 6D object pose estimation by modeling the conditional pose distribution with depth image input. The existing methods, however, suffer from slow convergence during training, learning its encoder with the diffusion denoising network in end-to-end fashion, and require an additional network that evaluates sampled pose hypotheses to filter out low-quality pose candidates. In this paper, we propose a novel pipeline that tackles these limitations by two key components. First, the proposed method pretrains the encoder with the direct pose regression head, and jointly learns the networks via the regression head and the denoising diffusion head, significantly accelerating training convergence while achieving higher accuracy. Second, sampling guidance via time-dependent score scaling is proposed s.t. the exploration-exploitation trade-off is effectively taken, eliminating the need for the additional evaluation network. The sampling guidance maintains multi-modal characteristics of symmetric objects at early denoising steps while ensuring high-quality pose generation at final steps. Extensive experiments on multiple benchmarks including REAL275, HouseCat6D, and ROPE, demonstrate that the proposed method, simple yet effective, achieves state-of-the-art accuracies even with single-pose inference, while being more efficient in both training and inference."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04125","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:06:50Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:06:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:06:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04069","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04069","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04069","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongyin","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Zongyin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qing","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Qing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diffusion Low Rank Hybrid Reconstruction for Sparse View Medical Imaging"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T07:20:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:50:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work presents TV-LoRA, a novel method for low-dose sparse-view CT reconstruction that combines a diffusion generative prior (NCSN++ with SDE modeling) and multi-regularization constraints, including anisotropic TV and nuclear norm (LoRA), within an ADMM framework. To address ill-posedness and texture loss under extremely sparse views, TV-LoRA integrates generative and physical constraints, and utilizes a 2D slice-based strategy with FFT acceleration and tensor-parallel optimization for efficient inference. Experiments on AAPM-2016, CTHD, and LIDC datasets with $N_{\\mathrm{view}}=8,4,2$ show that TV-LoRA consistently surpasses benchmarks in SSIM, texture recovery, edge clarity, and artifact suppression, demonstrating strong robustness and generalizability. Ablation studies confirm the complementary effects of LoRA regularization and diffusion priors, while the FFT-PCG module provides a speedup. Overall, Diffusion + TV-LoRA achieves high-fidelity, efficient 3D CT reconstruction and broad clinical applicability in low-dose, sparse-sampling scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04069","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:26Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:05:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04041","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04041","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04041","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayudh","familyName":"Saxena","name":"Saxena, Ayudh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harsh","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Harsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandeep","familyName":"Routray","name":"Routray, Sandeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishi Rajesh","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Rishi Rajesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Esha","familyName":"Pahwa","name":"Pahwa, Esha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SITCOM: Scaling Inference-Time COMpute for VLAs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T05:24:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:48:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Learning robust robotic control policies remains a major challenge due to the high cost of collecting labeled data, limited generalization to unseen environments, and difficulties in planning over long horizons. While Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models offer a promising solution by grounding natural language instructions into single-step control commands, they often lack mechanisms for lookahead and struggle with compounding errors in dynamic tasks. In this project, we introduce Scaling Inference-Time COMpute for VLAs (SITCOM), a framework that augments any pretrained VLA with model-based rollouts and reward-based trajectory selection, inspired by Model Predictive Control algorithm. SITCOM leverages a learned dynamics model to simulate multi-step action rollouts to select the best candidate plan for real-world execution, transforming one-shot VLAs into robust long-horizon planners. We develop an efficient transformer-based dynamics model trained on large-scale BridgeV2 data and fine-tuned on SIMPLER environments to bridge the Real2Sim gap, and score candidate rollouts using rewards from simulator. Through comprehensive evaluation across multiple tasks and settings in the SIMPLER environment, we demonstrate that SITCOM when combined with a good reward function can significantly improve task completion rate from 48% to 72% using trained dynamics model."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at the NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on Space in Vision, Language, and Embodied AI (SpaVLE). *Equal contribution"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04041","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03962","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03962","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03962","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanzhe","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Hanzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jialin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry","familyName":"Leung","name":"Leung, Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steve","familyName":"Drew","name":"Drew, Steve","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SPEAR: Soft Prompt Enhanced Anomaly Recognition for Time Series Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T22:20:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:44:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Time series anomaly detection plays a crucial role in a wide range of fields, such as healthcare and internet traffic monitoring. The emergence of large language models (LLMs) offers new opportunities for detecting anomalies in the ubiquitous time series data. Traditional approaches struggle with variable-length time series sequences and context-based anomalies. We propose Soft Prompt Enhanced Anomaly Recognition (SPEAR), a novel approach to leverage LLMs for anomaly detection with soft prompts and quantization. Our methodology involves quantizing and transforming the time series data into input embeddings and combining them with learnable soft prompt embeddings. These combined embeddings are then fed into a frozen LLM. The soft prompts are updated iteratively based on a cross-entropy loss, allowing the model to adapt to time series anomaly detection. The use of soft prompts helps adapt LLMs effectively to time series tasks, while quantization ensures optimal handling of sequences, as LLMs are designed to handle discrete sequences. Our experimental results demonstrate that soft prompts effectively increase LLMs' performance in downstream tasks regarding time series anomaly detection."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to 2025 IEEE International Conference on Autonomous and Trusted Computing (ATC 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03962","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:02:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:02:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:02:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03909","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03909","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03909","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyelin","familyName":"Nam","name":"Nam, Hyelin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyojun","familyName":"Go","name":"Go, Hyojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byeongjun","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Byeongjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byung-Hoon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Byung-Hoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyungjin","familyName":"Chung","name":"Chung, Hyungjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generating Human Motion Videos using a Cascaded Text-to-Video Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T19:16:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:40:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Human video generation is becoming an increasingly important task with broad applications in graphics, entertainment, and embodied AI. Despite the rapid progress of video diffusion models (VDMs), their use for general-purpose human video generation remains underexplored, with most works constrained to image-to-video setups or narrow domains like dance videos. In this work, we propose CAMEO, a cascaded framework for general human motion video generation. It seamlessly bridges Text-to-Motion (T2M) models and conditional VDMs, mitigating suboptimal factors that may arise in this process across both training and inference through carefully designed components. Specifically, we analyze and prepare both textual prompts and visual conditions to effectively train the VDM, ensuring robust alignment between motion descriptions, conditioning signals, and the generated videos. Furthermore, we introduce a camera-aware conditioning module that connects the two stages, automatically selecting viewpoints aligned with the input text to enhance coherence and reduce manual intervention. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach on both the MovieGen benchmark and a newly introduced benchmark tailored to the T2M-VDM combination, while highlighting its versatility across diverse use cases."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 7 figures, Project Page:https://hyelinnam.github.io/Cameo/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03909","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:31Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:01:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03847","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03847","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03847","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raghav","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Raghav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manan","familyName":"Mehta","name":"Mehta, Manan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Small Language Models for Agentic Systems: A Survey of Architectures, Capabilities, and Deployment Trade offs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T15:48:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:37:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Small language models (SLMs; 1-12B params, sometimes up to 20B) are sufficient and often superior for agentic workloads where the objective is schema- and API-constrained accuracy rather than open-ended generation. We synthesize recent evidence across open and proprietary SLMs (Phi-4-Mini, Qwen-2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Llama-3.2-1B/3B, Ministral-3B/8B, Apple on-device 3B, DeepSeek-R1-Distill) and connect it to modern evaluations (BFCL v3/v4, StableToolBench) and serving stacks (vLLM, SGLang, TensorRT-LLM) paired with guided decoding libraries (XGrammar, Outlines). We formalize SLM-default, LLM-fallback systems with uncertainty-aware routing and verifier cascades, and propose engineering metrics that reflect real production goals: cost per successful task (CPS), schema validity rate, executable call rate, p50/p95 latency, and energy per request. Guided decoding, strict JSON Schema outputs, and validator-first tool execution close much of the capability gap with larger models and often let SLMs match or surpass LLMs on tool use, function calling, and RAG at 10x-100x lower token cost with materially better latency and energy. We provide design patterns for agent stacks that prioritize SLMs: schema-first prompting, type-safe function registries, confidence scoring with verifier rollups, and lightweight adaptation via LoRA/QLoRA. We also delineate limits where fallback remains valuable (open-domain reasoning and some long-horizon planning). The result is a practical blueprint for building fast, inexpensive, and reliable agents that default to SLMs while preserving headroom with targeted LLM assistance.\n Keywords: small language models, agents, function calling, structured outputs, JSON Schema, guided decoding, LoRA/QLoRA, routing, energy efficiency, edge inference"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 Pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03847","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:59:54Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:59:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:59:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03821","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03821","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03821","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Venkata Narendra","familyName":"Kotyada","name":"Kotyada, Venkata Narendra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Revanth","familyName":"Eranki","name":"Eranki, Revanth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nagesh Bhattu","familyName":"Sristy","name":"Sristy, Nagesh Bhattu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Contrastive-SDE: Guiding Stochastic Differential Equations with Contrastive Learning for Unpaired Image-to-Image Translation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T14:37:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:35:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Unpaired image-to-image translation involves learning mappings between source domain and target domain in the absence of aligned or corresponding samples. Score based diffusion models have demonstrated state-of-the-art performance in generative tasks. Their ability to approximate complex data distributions through stochastic differential equations (SDEs) enables them to generate high-fidelity and diverse outputs, making them particularly well-suited for unpaired I2I settings. In parallel, contrastive learning provides a powerful framework for learning semantic similarities without the need for explicit supervision or paired data. By pulling together representations of semantically similar samples and pushing apart dissimilar ones, contrastive methods are inherently aligned with the objectives of unpaired translation. Its ability to selectively enforce semantic consistency at the feature level makes contrastive learning particularly effective for guiding generation in unpaired scenarios. In this work, we propose a time-dependent contrastive learning approach where a model is trained with SimCLR by considering an image and its domain invarient feature as a positive pair, enabling the preservation of domain-invariant features and the discarding of domain-specific ones. The learned contrastive model then guides the inference of a pretrained SDE for the I2I translation task. We empirically compare Contrastive-SDE with several baselines across three common unpaired I2I tasks, using four metrics for evaluation. Constrastive-SDE achieves comparable results to the state-of-the-art on several metrics. Furthermore, we observe that our model converges significantly faster and requires no label supervision or classifier training, making it a more efficient alternative for this task."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03821","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:59:15Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:59:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:59:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03760","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03760","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03760","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Chenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhichao","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Zhichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingfu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qingfu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EvoEngineer: Mastering Automated CUDA Kernel Code Evolution with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T10:00:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:30:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"CUDA kernel optimization has become a critical bottleneck for AI performance, as deep learning training and inference efficiency directly depends on highly optimized GPU kernels.\n Despite the promise of Large Language Models (LLMs) for automating kernel optimization, this field suffers from a fragmented ecosystem of isolated and incomparable approaches with unclear problem formulations.\n Furthermore, general-purpose LLM code evolution methods cannot meet strict correctness requirements of CUDA kernel optimization.\n We address these fundamental challenges by first formalizing CUDA kernel optimization as a code optimization task with a clear objective, constraints, and evaluation metrics.\n We then establish the first systematic LLM-based code evolution framework, EvoEngineer, that provides guidance for designing and adapting optimization strategies to achieve a balance between performance and correctness.\n Finally, we implement a kernel optimization system based on this framework and conduct extensive experiments on 91 real-world CUDA kernels.\n Our results demonstrate that EvoEngineer achieves a principled balance between performance and correctness, with the highest averaged median speedup of \\textbf{2.72}$\\times$ over baseline CUDA kernels and a code validity rate of \\textbf{69.8}\\%, outperforming existing methods on both dimensions.\n Our method achieves a maximum speedup of \\textbf{36.75}$\\times$ among all operations over PyTorch kernels and delivers the highest speedup on \\textbf{28} (\\textbf{56.0\\%}) of 50 operations that achieve over \\textbf{2$\\times$} acceleration."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under Review of ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03760","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:57:47Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:57:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:57:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03744","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03744","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03744","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianfei","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Qianfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayu","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Jiayu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peijun","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Peijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wensheng","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Wensheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meie","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Meie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HydroFusion-LMF: Semi-Supervised Multi-Network Fusion with Large-Model Adaptation for Long-Term Daily Runoff Forecasting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Geophysics (physics.geo-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T09:09:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:29:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate decade-scale daily runoff forecasting in small watersheds is difficult because signals blend drifting trends, multi-scale seasonal cycles, regime shifts, and sparse extremes. Prior deep models (DLinear, TimesNet, PatchTST, TiDE, Nonstationary Transformer, LSTNet, LSTM) usually target single facets and under-utilize unlabeled spans, limiting regime adaptivity. We propose HydroFusion-LMF, a unified framework that (i) performs a learnable trend-seasonal-residual decomposition to reduce non-stationarity, (ii) routes residuals through a compact heterogeneous expert set (linear refinement, frequency kernel, patch Transformer, recurrent memory, dynamically normalized attention), (iii) fuses expert outputs via a hydrologic context-aware gate conditioned on day-of-year phase, antecedent precipitation, local variance, flood indicators, and static basin attributes, and (iv) augments supervision with a semi-supervised multi-task objective (composite MSE/MAE + extreme emphasis + NSE/KGE, masked reconstruction, multi-scale contrastive alignment, augmentation consistency, variance-filtered pseudo-labeling). Optional adapter / LoRA layers inject a frozen foundation time-series encoder efficiently. On a ~10-year daily dataset HydroFusion-LMF attains MSE 1.0128 / MAE 0.5818, improving the strongest baseline (DLinear) by 10.2% / 10.3% and the mean baseline by 24.6% / 17.1%. We observe simultaneous MSE and MAE reductions relative to baselines. The framework balances interpretability (explicit components, sparse gating) with performance, advancing label-efficient hydrologic forecasting under non-stationarity."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"V1"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03744","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:57:23Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:57:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:57:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03743","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03743","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03743","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zachary","familyName":"Eberhart","name":"Eberhart, Zachary","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Collin","familyName":"McMillan","name":"McMillan, Collin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"APIDA-Chat: Structured Synthesis of API Search Dialogues to Bootstrap Conversational Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T08:58:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:29:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-language-model assistants are suitable for explaining popular APIs, yet they falter on niche or proprietary libraries because the multi-turn dialogue data needed for fine-tuning are scarce. We present APIDA-Chat, an open-source pipeline that converts symbolic dialogue-act \"scripts\" into realistic, domain-grounded API Search conversations using a lightweight model for inexpensive training data generation. Phase I pairs a legacy dialogue planner with a high-capability teacher LLM (o4-mini) to synthesize a \"gold set\" of realized dialogues; then, a smaller Llama 3.2 3B student model is fine-tuned on this corpus. Phase II drops the teacher and reuses the same planner with the fine-tuned model, allowing rapid, low-cost synthesis of new dialogues without exposing source code to external services. The fine-tuned student improves BLEU from 0.38 to 0.50 and BERTScore from 0.88 to 0.91 versus the base model while running entirely on a single consumer GPU. All components are modular and publicly released to serve as a conservative baseline for future work. APIDA-Chat is open-sourced at https://github.com/Zeberhart/apida-chat and a video demo is available at https://youtu.be/YqmZBHyGbPs ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"4 pages, 2 figures. To be published in Proceedings of the 40th IEEE/ACM International Conference on Automated Software Engineering"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03743","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:57:22Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:57:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:57:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03561","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03561","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03561","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Filipek","name":"Filipek, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reactive Transformer (RxT) -- Stateful Real-Time Processing for Event-Driven Reactive Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T23:18:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:14:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Transformer architecture has become the de facto standard for Large Language Models (LLMs), demonstrating remarkable capabilities in language understanding and generation. However, its application in conversational AI is fundamentally constrained by its stateless nature and the quadratic computational complexity ($O(L^2)$) with respect to sequence length $L$. Current models emulate memory by reprocessing an ever-expanding conversation history with each turn, leading to prohibitive costs and latency in long dialogues. This paper introduces the Reactive Transformer (RxT), a novel architecture designed to overcome these limitations by shifting from a data-driven to an event-driven paradigm. RxT processes each conversational turn as a discrete event in real-time, maintaining context in an integrated, fixed-size Short-Term Memory (STM) system. The architecture features a distinct operational cycle where a generator-decoder produces a response based on the current query and the previous memory state, after which a memory-encoder and a dedicated Memory Attention network asynchronously update the STM with a representation of the complete interaction. This design fundamentally alters the scaling dynamics, reducing the total user-facing cost of a conversation from quadratic ($O(N^2 \\cdot T)$) to linear ($O(N \\cdot T)$) with respect to the number of interactions $N$. By decoupling response generation from memory updates, RxT achieves low latency, enabling truly real-time, stateful, and economically viable long-form conversations. We validated our architecture with a series of proof-of-concept experiments on synthetic data, demonstrating superior performance and constant-time inference latency compared to a baseline stateless model of comparable size."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 13 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03561","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:52:55Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:52:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:52:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03541","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03541","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03541","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Halterman","name":"Halterman, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katherine A.","familyName":"Keith","name":"Keith, Katherine A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What is a protest anyway? Codebook conceptualization is still a first-order concern in LLM-era classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T22:19:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:13:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative large language models (LLMs) are now used extensively for text classification in computational social science (CSS). In this work, focus on the steps before and after LLM prompting -- conceptualization of concepts to be classified and using LLM predictions in downstream statistical inference -- which we argue have been overlooked in much of LLM-era CSS. We claim LLMs can tempt analysts to skip the conceptualization step, creating conceptualization errors that bias downstream estimates. Using simulations, we show that this conceptualization-induced bias cannot be corrected for solely by increasing LLM accuracy or post-hoc bias correction methods. We conclude by reminding CSS analysts that conceptualization is still a first-order concern in the LLM-era and provide concrete advice on how to pursue low-cost, unbiased, low-variance downstream estimates."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03541","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:52:25Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:52:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:52:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03515","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03515","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03515","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lianghuan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Lianghuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sagnik","familyName":"Anupam","name":"Anupam, Sagnik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Insup","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Insup","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Osbert","familyName":"Bastani","name":"Bastani, Osbert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RAPID: An Efficient Reinforcement Learning Algorithm for Small Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T20:58:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:10:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has emerged as a promising strategy for finetuning small language models (SLMs) to solve targeted tasks such as math and coding. However, RL algorithms tend to be resource-intensive, taking a significant amount of time to train. We propose RAPID, a novel RL algorithm that can substantially reduce the running time of RL. Our key insight is that RL tends to be costly due to the need to perform both inference and backpropagation during training. To maximize use of computational resources, our algorithm performs inference in large batches, and then performs off-policy policy gradient updates in mini-batches. For off-policy updates, we incorporate group advantage estimation into the policy gradient algorithm, and derive an importance weighted estimator to correct for the bias arising from off-policy learning. Our experiments demonstrate that our algorithm can reduce running time by 11%-34% on three benchmarks compared to state-of-the-art RL algorithms while maintaining similar or better accuracy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03515","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:51:48Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:51:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:51:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03425","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03425","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03425","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Congzheng","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Congzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Memory-Efficient Backpropagation for Fine-Tuning LLMs on Resource-Constrained Mobile Devices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T18:36:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:06:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fine-tuning large language models (LLMs) with backpropagation\\textemdash even for a subset of parameters such as LoRA\\textemdash can be much more memory-consuming than inference and is often deemed impractical for resource-constrained mobile devices. Alternative methods, such as zeroth-order optimization (ZO), can greatly reduce the memory footprint but come at the cost of significantly slower model convergence (10$\\times$ to 100$\\times$ more steps than backpropagation). We propose a memory-efficient implementation of backpropagation (MeBP) on mobile devices that provides better trade-off between memory usage and compute time, while converging faster and achieving better performance than the ZO baseline. We verify the effectiveness of MeBP on an iPhone 15 Pro Max and show that various LLMs, ranging from 0.5B to 4B parameters, can be fine-tuned using less than 1GB of memory. We release an example of the MeBP implementation at https://github.com/apple/ml-mebp."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03425","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:49:36Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:49:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:49:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03371","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03371","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03371","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sasho","familyName":"Nedelkoski","name":"Nedelkoski, Sasho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Acker","name":"Acker, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Odej","familyName":"Kao","name":"Kao, Odej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soeren","familyName":"Becker","name":"Becker, Soeren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dominik","familyName":"Scheinert","name":"Scheinert, Dominik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Distributed Low-Communication Training with Decoupled Momentum Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T08:25:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:03:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The training of large models demands substantial computational resources, typically available only in data centers with high-bandwidth interconnects. However, reducing the reliance on high-bandwidth interconnects between nodes enables the use of distributed compute resources as an alternative to centralized data center training. Building on recent advances in distributed model training, we propose an approach that further reduces communication by combining infrequent synchronizations across distributed model replicas with gradient momentum compression. In particular, we treat the optimizer momentum as a signal and decompose the Nesterov momentum into high- and low-frequency components via the discrete cosine transform (DCT). Only the high-frequency components are synchronized across model replicas every $H$ steps. Empirically, our method achieves up to a $16\\times$ reduction in communication compared to the baseline DiLoCo, and it generalizes across architectures, including transformer-based language models and convolutional neural networks for images. Overall, this work advances the feasibility of training large models on distributed nodes with low-bandwidth interconnects."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 - DynaFront 2025: Dynamics at the Frontiers of Optimization, Sampling, and Games Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03371","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:48:17Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:48:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:48:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03364","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03364","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03364","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolong","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashley","familyName":"Tarrant","name":"Tarrant, Ashley","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rao","familyName":"Kotamarthi","name":"Kotamarthi, Rao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiali","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajkumar","familyName":"Kettimuthu","name":"Kettimuthu, Rajkumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diffusion-Based, Data-Assimilation-Enabled Super-Resolution of Hub-height Winds"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T03:38:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:03:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-quality observations of hub-height winds are valuable but sparse in space and time. Simulations are widely available on regular grids but are generally biased and too coarse to inform wind-farm siting or to assess extreme-weather-related risks (e.g., gusts) at infrastructure scales. To fully utilize both data types for generating high-quality, high-resolution hub-height wind speeds (tens to ~100m above ground), this study introduces WindSR, a diffusion model with data assimilation for super-resolution downscaling of hub-height winds. WindSR integrates sparse observational data with simulation fields during downscaling using state-of-the-art diffusion models. A dynamic-radius blending method is introduced to merge observations with simulations, providing conditioning for the diffusion process. Terrain information is incorporated during both training and inference to account for its role as a key driver of winds. Evaluated against convolutional-neural-network and generative-adversarial-network baselines, WindSR outperforms them in both downscaling efficiency and accuracy. Our data assimilation reduces WindSR's model bias by approximately 20% relative to independent observations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03364","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:48:07Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:48:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:48:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03358","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03358","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03358","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Annan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Annan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danielle C.","familyName":"Maddix","name":"Maddix, Danielle C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boran","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Boran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdul Fatir","familyName":"Ansari","name":"Ansari, Abdul Fatir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oleksandr","familyName":"Shchur","name":"Shchur, Oleksandr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christos","familyName":"Faloutsos","name":"Faloutsos, Christos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew Gordon","familyName":"Wilson","name":"Wilson, Andrew Gordon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael W.","familyName":"Mahoney","name":"Mahoney, Michael W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Understanding Transformers for Time Series: Rank Structure, Flow-of-ranks, and Compressibility"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T23:56:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:03:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers are widely used across data modalities, and yet the principles distilled from text models often transfer imperfectly to models trained to other modalities. In this paper, we analyze Transformers through the lens of rank structure. Our focus is on the time series setting, where the structural properties of the data differ remarkably from those of text or vision. We show that time-series embeddings, unlike text or vision, exhibit sharply decaying singular value spectra: small patch sizes and smooth continuous mappings concentrate the data into low-rank subspaces. From this, we prove that the associated $Q/K/V$ projections admit accurate low-rank approximations, and that attention layers become compressible in proportion to the decay of the embedding spectrum. We introduce the concept of flow-of-ranks, a phenomenon by which nonlinear mixing across depth inflates the rank, explaining why early layers are most amenable to compression and why ranks grow with depth. Guided by these theoretical and empirical results, we use these insights to compress Chronos, a large time series foundation model, achieving a reduction of $65\\%$ in inference time and $81\\%$ in memory, without loss of accuracy. Our findings provide principled guidance for allocating width, depth, and heads in time series foundation models, and for exploiting their inherent compressibility."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"42 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03358","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:47:58Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:47:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:47:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03334","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03334","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03334","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zerui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zerui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinghao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Qinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ana","familyName":"Klimovic","name":"Klimovic, Ana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianwei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonggang","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Yonggang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dahua","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Dahua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Semantic-Aware Scheduling for GPU Clusters with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T02:01:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:02:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep learning (DL) schedulers are pivotal in optimizing resource allocation in GPU clusters, but operate with a critical limitation: they are largely blind to the semantic context of the jobs they manage. This forces them to rely on limited metadata, leading to high profiling overhead, unreliable duration estimation, inadequate failure handling, and poor observability. To this end, we propose SchedMate, a framework that bridges this semantic gap by systematically extracting deep insights from overlooked, unstructured data sources: source code, runtime logs, and historical jobs. SchedMate enhances existing schedulers non-intrusively through three LLM-based components. Our implementation integrates seamlessly with existing deep learning schedulers. Evaluations on a 128-GPU physical cluster and extensive simulations on production traces show SchedMate reduces average job completion times by up to 1.91x, substantially enhancing the scheduling performance, demonstrating the critical role of semantic-awareness in modern DL scheduling."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03334","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:47:24Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:47:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:47:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03284","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03284","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03284","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vinay","familyName":"Venkatesh","name":"Venkatesh, Vinay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vamsidhar R","familyName":"Kamanuru","name":"Kamanuru, Vamsidhar R","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lav","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Lav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikita","familyName":"Kothari","name":"Kothari, Nikita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Edge-FIT: Federated Instruction Tuning of Quantized LLMs for Privacy-Preserving Smart Home Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-28T20:06:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper proposes Edge-FIT (Federated Instruction Tuning on the Edge), a scalable framework for Federated Instruction Tuning (FIT) of Large Language Models (LLMs). Traditional Federated Learning (TFL) methods, like FedAvg, fail when confronted with the massive parameter size of LLMs [3], [6]. Our Edge-FIT framework combines federated learning with 4-bit Quantized Low-Rank Adaptation (QLORA), mitigating the core issues of communication and computational overhead. We demonstrate this by filtering the general-purpose Databricks Dolly 15k dataset for the IoT domain. Experimental results show the Edge-FIT tuned Llama 2(7B) achieves an F1-Score of 0.89. We also demonstrate a viable trade-off using the 3.8B Phi-3-mini model, validating Edge-FIT as a scalable framework for decentralized LLM deployment on home compute gateways."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03284","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:10Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:46:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03283","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03283","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03283","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MACE: A Hybrid LLM Serving System with Colocated SLO-aware Continuous Retraining Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-28T18:45:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) deployed on edge servers are increasingly used in latency-sensitive applications such as personalized assistants, recommendation, and content moderation. However, the non-stationary nature of user data necessitates frequent retraining, which introduces a fundamental tension between inference latency and model accuracy under constrained GPU resources. Existing retraining strategies either delay model updates, over-commit resources to retraining, or overlook iteration-level retraining granularity. In this paper, we identify that iteration-level scheduling is crucial for adapting retraining frequency to model drift without violating service-level objectives (SLOs). We propose MACE, a hybrid LLM system that colocates concurrent inference (prefill, decode) and fine-tuning, with intelligent memory management to maximize task performance while promising inference throughput. MACE leverages the insight that not all model updates equally affect output alignment and allocates GPU cycles accordingly to balance throughput, latency, and update freshness. Our trace-driven evaluation shows that MACE matches or exceeds continuous retraining while reducing inference latency by up to 63% and maintaining throughput under resource constraints. Compared to periodic retraining, MACE improves latency breakdown across prefill, decode, and finetune stages, and sustains GPU utilization above 85% in NVIDIA AGX Orin. These results demonstrate that iteration-level hybrid scheduling is a promising direction for deploying LLMs with continual learning capabilities on edge platforms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 15 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03283","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:46:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03279","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03279","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03279","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youjin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Youjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangjingyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yangjingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxuan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jiaxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MemMamba: Rethinking Memory Patterns in State Space Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-28T14:40:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the explosive growth of data, long-sequence modeling has become increasingly important in tasks such as natural language processing and bioinformatics. However, existing methods face inherent trade-offs between efficiency and memory. Recurrent neural networks suffer from gradient vanishing and explosion, making them hard to scale. Transformers can model global dependencies but are constrained by quadratic complexity. Recently, selective state-space models such as Mamba have demonstrated high efficiency with O(n) time and O(1) recurrent inference, yet their long-range memory decays exponentially. In this work, we conduct mathematical derivations and information-theoretic analysis to systematically uncover the memory decay mechanism of Mamba, answering a fundamental question: what is the nature of Mamba's long-range memory and how does it retain information? To quantify key information loss, we further introduce horizontal-vertical memory fidelity metrics that capture degradation both within and across layers. Inspired by how humans distill and retain salient information when reading long documents, we propose MemMamba, a novel architectural framework that integrates state summarization mechanism together with cross-layer and cross-token attention, which alleviates long-range forgetting while preserving linear complexity. MemMamba achieves significant improvements over existing Mamba variants and Transformers on long-sequence benchmarks such as PG19 and Passkey Retrieval, while delivering a 48% speedup in inference efficiency. Both theoretical analysis and empirical results demonstrate that MemMamba achieves a breakthrough in the complexity-memory trade-off, offering a new paradigm for ultra-long sequence modeling."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03279","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:02Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:46:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03275","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03275","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03275","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhao","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Junhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Limin","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Limin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiujun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SDQ-LLM: Sigma-Delta Quantization for 1-bit LLMs of any size"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T14:49:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) face significant computational and memory challenges, making extremely low-bit quantization crucial for their efficient deployment. In this work, we introduce SDQ-LLM: Sigma-Delta Quantization for 1-bit LLMs of any size, a novel framework that enables extremely low-bit quantization of LLMs while preserving their linguistic reasoning capabilities. A distinctive feature of SDQ-LLM is the continuous adjustability of the Over-Sampling Ratio (OSR), enabling dynamic adaptation to memory or VRAM constraints by selecting fractional OSR (e.g. 2.5 times) for an optimal trade-off between model size and accuracy. SDQ-LLM uses upsampling combined with Sigma-Delta Quantizer to binarize or ternarize LLMs weights, encoding high-precision parameters into 1-bit or 1.58-bit representations, replacing the multiplication operations within linear layers with addition. This approach significantly enhances inference efficiency under extremely low-bit quantization. To further reduce the loss of quantization precision, we incorporate Hadamard-based weight smoothing prior to quantization, improving the stability and robustness of the weight representations. Furthermore, to fully leverage the continuity of the OSR and reduce precision loss, recognizing the correlation between quantization sensitivity and weight variance, we propose a fine-grained, layer- and linear-wise OSR allocation strategy, MultiOSR. This strategy distributes OSR both across layers and within each layer, based on weight variance and parameter scale. Finally, extensive experiments on OPT and LLaMA model families demonstrate that SDQ-LLM achieves a more efficient and high-precision performance even under highly aggressive low-OSR settings. Our code is available at https://github.com/Dreamlittlecat/LLM-Quant-Factory."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03275","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:56Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:45:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03274","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03274","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03274","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiteng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianglong","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Xianglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotong","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Haotong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yulun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quant-dLLM: Post-Training Extreme Low-Bit Quantization for Diffusion Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T13:50:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion large language models (dLLMs), which offer bidirectional context and flexible masked-denoising generation, are emerging as a compelling alternative to autoregressive (AR) LLMs. However, like AR LLMs, their model sizes continue to grow, motivating weight compression for deployment. Although post-training quantization (PTQ) is effective for AR LLMs, directly transferring it to dLLMs at 2-bit leads to unsatisfactory performance. To tackle these challenges, we propose Quant-dLLM, an ultra-low-bit PTQ framework tailored to dLLMs. Since masked-denoising activations in dLLMs differ from the fully visible signals assumed by standard PTQ methods, we introduce Masked Calibration Simulation (MCS) to align calibration with the timestep-dependent masking, which yields more reliable calibrations. Moreover, we propose a Data-aware Any-order Quantizer (DAQ) that learns ultra-low-bit weight representations via an optimization algorithm. It performs iterative approximation guided by our simulated calibration data. In addition, under a strict 2-bit budget, we introduce Adaptive Blockwise Mixed Precision (ABMP), a sensitivity-based precision allocation scheme that adaptively assigns bit width across channel groups. When restricted to 2-bit precision, Quant-dLLM consistently achieves higher accuracy than state-of-the-art (SOTA) AR-transfer PTQ methods on dLLMs. The code and models will be available at: https://github.com/ZTA2785/Quant-dLLM."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03274","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:54Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:45:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03270","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03270","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03270","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haolin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Haolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zuxin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jielin","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Jielin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianguo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingbo","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yingbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zeyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shelby","familyName":"Heinecke","name":"Heinecke, Shelby","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Silvio","familyName":"Savarese","name":"Savarese, Silvio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiming","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Caiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiran","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Weiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CoDA: Coding LM via Diffusion Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T05:41:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion language models promise bidirectional context and infilling capabilities that autoregressive coders lack, yet practical systems remain heavyweight. We introduce CoDA, a 1.7B-parameter diffusion coder trained on TPU with a fully open-source training pipeline. CoDA pairs large-scale diffusion pre-training with code-centric mid-training and instruction tuning, enabling confidence-guided sampling that keeps inference latency competitive. On Humaneval, MBPP, and EvalPlus, CoDA-1.7B-Instruct matches or surpasses diffusion models up to 7B parameters. Our release includes model checkpoints, evaluation harnesses, and TPU training pipelines to accelerate research on lightweight diffusion-based coding assistants."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03270","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:48Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:45:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03261","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03261","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03261","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"C.","familyName":"Coelho","name":"Coelho, C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Hohmann","name":"Hohmann, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D.","familyName":"Fernández","name":"Fernández, D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L.","familyName":"Penter","name":"Penter, L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S.","familyName":"Ihlenfeldt","name":"Ihlenfeldt, S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"O.","familyName":"Niggemann","name":"Niggemann, O.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Data-Driven Temperature Modelling of Machine Tools by Neural Networks: A Benchmark"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"J.2; I.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T15:43:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:00:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Thermal errors in machine tools significantly impact machining precision and productivity. Traditional thermal error correction/compensation methods rely on measured temperature-deformation fields or on transfer functions. Most existing data-driven compensation strategies employ neural networks (NNs) to directly predict thermal errors or specific compensation values. While effective, these approaches are tightly bound to particular error types, spatial locations, or machine configurations, limiting their generality and adaptability. In this work, we introduce a novel paradigm in which NNs are trained to predict high-fidelity temperature and heat flux fields within the machine tool. The proposed framework enables subsequent computation and correction of a wide range of error types using modular, swappable downstream components. The NN is trained using data obtained with the finite element method under varying initial conditions and incorporates a correlation-based selection strategy that identifies the most informative measurement points, minimising hardware requirements during inference. We further benchmark state-of-the-art time-series NN architectures, namely Recurrent NN, Gated Recurrent Unit, Long-Short Term Memory (LSTM), Bidirectional LSTM, Transformer, and Temporal Convolutional Network, by training both specialised models, tailored for specific initial conditions, and general models, capable of extrapolating to unseen scenarios. The results show accurate and low-cost prediction of temperature and heat flux fields, laying the basis for enabling flexible and generalisable thermal error correction in machine tool environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03261","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:35Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:45:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03246","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03246","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03246","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangji","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Guangji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StructPrune: Structured Global Pruning asymptotics with $\\mathcal{O}(\\sqrt{N})$ GPU Memory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-25T19:16:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:00:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pruning is critical for scaling large language models (LLMs). Global pruning achieves strong performance but requires $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ memory, which is infeasible for billion-parameter models. Local pruning reduces GPU memory usage to that of a single layer by pruning layers independently, but it neglects inter-layer dependencies and often leads to suboptimal performance in high-sparsity regimes. Unlike unstructured pruning, structured pruning produces regular sparsity patterns that align well with GPU kernels and library optimizations, making it more hardware-efficient. However, structured pruning typically relies on global pruning, since structured patterns are more prone to severe performance degradation under local optimization. To jointly achieve structured pruning and the memory efficiency of local pruning, we propose a divide-and-conquer strategy that decomposes the global pruning problem into coordinated subproblems across different modules, each of which fits within limited GPU memory. Building on this idea, we design \\textbf{STRUPRUNE}, an ADMM-based framework that integrates structured sparsity into the pruning process, combining the memory efficiency of local pruning with the hardware compatibility of structured methods. We derive a closed-form analytical solution for structured pruning masks that provides an explicit rule for layer-wise sparsity allocation, and further develop an energy-based asymptotic framework yielding a softmax-form allocation scheme that simplifies optimization while adapting to heterogeneous layer importance. Experiments demonstrate that STRUPRUNE matches the perplexity of global structured pruning while reducing memory cost from $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ to $\\mathcal{O}(\\sqrt{N})$, enabling practical deployment at the billion-parameter scale."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03246","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:13Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:45:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":90,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-06T16%3A00%3A00Z%20TO%202025-10-07T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D%20AND%20((GPU%20OR%20kernel%20OR%20inference%20OR%20serving%20OR%20quantization%20OR%20parallel%20OR%20cache%20OR%20communication)%20AND%20(%22language%20model%22%20OR%20%22language%20models%22%20OR%20transformer%20OR%20transformers%20OR%20diffusion))\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1\u0026sort=registered"}}